{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && git check-ignore -v cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n.gitignore:87:cores/*/implementation_notes.md\tcores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n# Registered load/store address\n\n- EX/MEM captures the full 32-bit sum of forwarded `rs1` and the decoded immediate under the existing stage enable. JALR uses the same sum and clears bit zero on its redirect target.\n- MEM uses the registered byte address for `io_dmemAddr`, alignment traps, load extraction, byte masks, and the aligned RVFI address. The integer ALU result still feeds fast bypass and writeback. The EX/MEM hold keeps the address, store data, and request controls stable until the bus accepts the transfer.\n- The three-file implementation follows the proposed design. With neither data request enable asserted, `io_dmemAddr` still follows the address register. The new field adds 32 registers compared with the champion. Verification used scratch copies under `$TMPDIR`.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All six cocotb test runners passed, including the pipeline checks for memory backpressure and retirement timing.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first run.\n- RVFI cosim passed on selftest and all three random programs with ready buses and with instruction and data stalls.\n- Stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 4,527,760 marker-window cycles. All five canonical CRCs and the UART validation message matched.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) | Logic cells | Registers |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 62.973 | 2,860 | 676 |\n| 1 | 68.323 | 2,860 | 676 |\n| 2 | 68.323 | 2,860 | 676 |\n\nThe worst setup path ended at the EX/MEM integer ALU result in all three placements. The median Fmax of 68.323 MHz exceeds the 59.94 MHz acceptance threshold and yields 150.90 CoreMark iterations/s, a 19.2% gain over the champion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
