{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_21", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    row = json.loads(line)\n    if 'hyp-20261123-001-r10s0' in [str(v) for v in row.values() if isinstance(v, str)]:\n        print(json.dumps(row, indent=2)[:12000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261123-001-r10s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Resolve load-dependent branches across EX and MEM\",\n  \"category\": \"micro_opt\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The bench champion completes CoreMark in 3,965,757 marker cycles at\\n97.408 MHz. A cycle profile counted 131,634 advancing load interlocks\\nwith a conditional branch as the consumer. The branch need only\\nresolve by the cycle in which the interlocked version would reach\\nEX. Eliminating the interlock by feeding the load directly into\\nEX recovery cut the cycles to 3,855,729 but routed at 61.115 MHz.\\nThe routed path ran from data RAM through load extraction and branch\\ncomparison to the fetch queue. The EX/MEM edge can split that path.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The bench champion completes CoreMark in 3,965,757 marker cycles at\\n97.408 MHz. A cycle profile counted 131,634 advancing load interlocks\\nwith a conditional branch as the consumer. The branch need only\\nresolve by the cycle in which the interlocked version would reach\\nEX. Eliminating the interlock by feeding the load directly into\\nEX recovery cut the cycles to 3,855,729 but routed at 61.115 MHz.\\nThe routed path ran from data RAM through load extraction and branch\\ncomparison to the fetch queue. The EX/MEM edge can split that path.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"When a valid load in EX has a nonzero destination that matches either\\ninput of a legal, word-aligned conditional branch in ID, issue that\\nbranch with registered late-operand match bits. Keep the normal\\ninterlock for stores, other consumers, RV32M results, and branches\\nwith potentially misaligned targets. In the next cycle, the load is\\nin MEM and the branch is in EX. Form the branch's direct target and\\nfall-through there. Keep the ordinary forwarded operands as fallback\\nin case the load traps. An older data-bus stall holds both instructions.\\n\\nCompute byte equality and unsigned less-than in parallel, then latch\\nthose flags and the sign bits in the branch's EX/MEM payload. Use the\\nformatted memory value only in the local byte comparisons.\n…[8929 of 12001 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d5c3d8433d5df672]…\ne requires `NRET=1` for this core's one-channel co-simulation build.\\n- `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` completed. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached **89.262, 99.867, and 99.867 MHz**, using 4,747 logic cells and 1,425 registers. The median and **3,858,757** marker cycles give **258.81 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **5.37%** from the supplied champion results. Placement option 0 remains the slowest and the median is 0.633 MHz below the proposed 100.5 MHz target.\\n- The unchanged single-retire formal gate on the final RTL passed 52 of 53 checks, including ready-bus liveness and both PC continuity checks. `reg_ch0` was still solving at the local time limit. No check reported a counterexample. The initial byte-flag implementation passed all 53 checks before the recovery and ALU timing revisions.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-23T05:03:39.523163Z\",\n  \"slot\": 0,\n  \"round_id\": 10,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r10s0 (improvement, +5.37%): Registering byte-wise equality and ordering flags at EX/ME\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
