{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_23", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && rg -n 'regress|divider|radix|negative multiples' cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl --max-count 9\"", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Fold the EX divider into six registered iterations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired DIVUs were outside it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired DIVUs were outside it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU with one unsigned restoring divider in EX.\\nOn launch, capture the forwarded operands, take unsigned magnitudes for signed\\noperations, and save the result kind and signs. Run six radix-2 steps per\\nclock for six clocks using a 36-bit dividend stream with four leading zeros.\\nEach step shifts in a dividend bit, takes a 33-bit trial remainder through a\\nborrow-producing subtraction of the divisor, and shifts the quotient bit into\\nthe vacated low end of the stream. Select and sign-correct the quotient or\\nremainder from registered \n…[80982 of 84054 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dbc1e8fb365f3747]…\nto a four-way EX mux left MEM/WB write qualification on the critical path to the fetch PC at a median 72.896 MHz, and registering the final one-hot forwarding selects and preselecting PC and immediate operands in ID won at 76.948 MHz with the same 4,527,760 timed CoreMark cycles.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:10:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +5.47%): Page-local branch and JAL prediction with registered recovery cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45%, and the critical path into the EX/MEM ALU result register limited median Gowin Fmax to 67.414 MHz, below the 72 MHz needed to match the design with decode-stage operand selection.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:11:- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r5s0 (regression, +12.59%): Parallel radix-16 trial subtractions removed the serial radix-4 remainder dependency and shifted the timing limit to EX-to-ID operand-selection feedback at 86.637 MHz, leaving the variant that registered full-width divisor multiples to win at 88.028 MHz with the same timed CoreMark cycle count.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
