{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_110","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,200p' implementation_notes.md && tail -n 2 \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/div-formal.jiuSxi14/final.log\"'","aggregated_output":"# Radix-16 divider implementation\n\nThe divider captures the dividend magnitude and a signed 33-bit negative divisor on issue. Four independent byte additions perform conditional negation. The leading-nibble update uses a captured positive low nibble and a greater-than-15 flag computed directly from the input divisor.\n\nSetup forms the seven negative odd multiples with 37-bit shifted terms and carry-save reduction. It stores their low 33 bits and one range flag per digit from the full-width multiple, after shifting for even digits. Each trial uses a native 33-bit addition. The range flag rejects out-of-range multiples, and the sum’s sign rejects negative residuals, including the exact `2^32` boundary. The original monotone residual mux tree and quotient encoder select the final result.\n\nThe leading-nibble calculation differs from the proposal. A four-case decode handles the first two bits, followed by two restoring steps in the same setup cycle. For the first two bits, the partial dividend is at most three, which makes the case decode equivalent to the two restoring steps it replaces. This reduced the setup logic and improved routed timing.\n\nThe EX issue and result handshake, original RVFI operands, MEM sign correction, and ALTOPS behavior retain the nine-cycle EX schedule. The divider tests now cover byte-boundary carries and negative divisors around the leading-nibble cutoff.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall` in both the real and ALTOPS builds.\n- All six cocotb suites passed, including arithmetic, divider handshakes, and the pipeline.\n- RVFI co-simulation passed on selftest and all three random programs with ready and stalled buses. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed all CRCs in 3,658,045 marker cycles.\n- The single-retire `formal/run_all.sh` gate completed twice with 53 passes and zero failures, using `wrapper_si.sv`, `checks_si.cfg`, and ALTOPS. The final divider produces the same preprocessed ALTOPS source as the staged formal run.\n- Scratch SAT proofs passed for issue capture and leading-nibble division, all seven widened odd products, and candidate residuals and fits for digits 1 through 15. The recurrence proof used arbitrary 32-bit trials and divisors accepted through the issue handshake.\n\n## Gowin measurements\n\n| Placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 100.050 MHz |\n| 1 | 104.028 MHz |\n| 2 | 104.028 MHz |\n\n`make fpga TARGET=bench` measured a 104.028 MHz median and 284.38 CoreMark iterations/s. That is 4.61% above the 99.444 MHz champion at the same cycle count and about 0.007 MHz above the replacement threshold. The median fell 0.401 MHz below the prototype. The median placement was limited by the path from the registered EX ALU result to the EX/MEM trap control, with remainder feedback 0.003 ns behind in data delay. The design used 5,326 logic cells and 1,824 registers.\n\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
