{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')\nfor line in p.read_text().splitlines()[-8:]:\n    row=json.loads(line)\n    print(row.get('id'),row.get('status'),row.get('title'))\n    for k in ('formal','fpga','fitness','implementation_notes'):\n        if k in row:\n            print(k,str(row[k])[:1200])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-002-r12s1 None Forward registered load responses to branches and address generation\nfitness 266.52\nimplementation_notes # Registered load forwarding and branch recovery\n\nDecode allows a load to use a preceding LW as its base at displacements 0, 1, and 2. It records both raw source matches, including the unused rs2 field. EX keeps the ordinary forwarded operands available for a trapping producer. MEM/WB captures the raw load word alongside the formatted result, and RVFI uses the qualified result for each matching source.\n\nGowin could not infer the data block RAM with a mux after the address registers. The implementation selects the ordinary address or response upper bits at the request register's input. The address port is driven directly by that register. A three-bit sum supplies the low bits and carry. A carry suppresses both strobes for one cycle and rewrites the request register's upper bits using the saved raw word. The pipeline holds the response during correction and holds the corrected request through backpressure. The regular width-dependent alignment check covers the final byte address.\n\nDeferred branches compare the registered formatted load result against the nonmatching EX operand, byte lane by byte lane. MEM accounts for signed ordering, reversed operands, two qualified matches, and the\nhyp-20261123-003-r12s2 None Replay predicted loops with registered recovery at both stage boundaries\nfitness 271.85\nimplementation_notes # Implementation notes\n\nMEM registers a replay observation for each completing instruction, including the resolved direction of deferred branches. A 32-word circular history tracks page-local sequential runs and identifies a span from the displacement of a short taken backward branch. Observations continue during a copy. The registered reader advances through the captured span before the writer can wrap onto it. One synchronous replay RAM holds both 16-word banks. Installation publishes the full head PC with the final write, prefers an empty bank before round-robin repla\n…[6347 of 9419 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:652dfb5c07c40295]…\nsters the cold direction, local branch, and local JAL flags from distributed RAM.\n\nThe tag and registered eight-bit row are checked against the current PC in parallel. Bank parity completes the address comparison. Fetch uses the steering lane for replay prediction and sends the matching payload into the four-entry queue. Bus priority, predictor training, lookahead, queue admission, and correction timing retain their existing behavior.\n\nThe even-bank lookahead increment computes its carries in parallel. Two-bit comparator groups test the high tag and sampled index. Reset and FENCE.I clear both validity maps and sampled availability. A pending write is suppressed on the same edge.\n\n## Local verification\n\nVerilator lint and all seven cocotb suites passed. Directed replay checks covered lane pairing, high-tag and sampled-row aliases, both parity crossings, same-edge replacement, and FENCE.I \nhyp-20261123-003-r14s2 None Prime instruction replay from the predicted branch target\nfitness 296.08\nimplementation_notes \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
