k(even)\n    await check(even_alias)\n    await edge(odd)\n    await check(even)\n    await check(even_alias, replacement)\n\n    await edge(odd, _update(odd_alias, replacement))\n    await check(odd)\n    await check(odd_alias)\n    await check(even_alias, replacement)\n    await edge(odd)\n    await check(odd)\n    await check(odd_alias, replacement)\n\n    await edge(odd, _update(odd, odd_instr), i\n…[12905 of 15977 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9d44ac587693d9d3]…\n** above the champion. Placement 0 used 5,952 logic cells and eight block RAMs including the benchmark wrapper. The slower option was limited by EX forwarding into the ALU. The median's worst reported path runs from a replay RAM output to a fetch PC register.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire checks on the final RTL. The first run hit a Yosys parser error on `$bits(replay_row_t)`. Using `$bits` on a signal of that type resolved it, and the retry passed. The formal wrapper holds both buses ready. Directed tests and stalled co-simulation exercise replay under backpressure.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-23T07:53:42.318275Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r13s2 (improvement, +7.17%): Full-PC-tagged instructions with stored direct targets let fetch replay beyond closed loops during instruction-bus stalls, with even/odd BRAM banks reading the current and next sequential PCs in parallel to cover both hold and advance and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.62%.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
