{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_78", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    entry = json.loads(line)\n    if entry.get('id') in ('hyp-20261123-001-r11s0','hyp-20261123-001-r13s0'):\n        print(entry['id'], entry['title'], entry.get('fmax_mhz'), entry.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r11s0 Compare raw load lanes before selecting branch bytes 101.325 # Compare raw load lanes before selecting branch bytes\n\nEX compares eight raw-memory byte pairs against the nonmatching forwarded branch operand. The existing EX/MEM advance captures their unsigned less and equal flags, the raw sign bits, and the operand's all-zero and all-one prefix tests. The load's registered width, address, and write eligibility produce the one-hot selectors without using the memory data. A trapping load or double match disables the payload comparisons.\n\nMEM combines each byte, halfword, and word candidate lexicographically, then selects the candidate. EX predecodes the signed prefix ordering and the branch inversion from metadata and the nonmatching operand. This lets MEM steer equal and less terms in parallel, including less-or-equal for reversed operand order. The ordinary EX comparison supplies the trapping-load fallback. The formatted MEM/WB result supplies the matching RVFI operands. The older MEM miss owns fetch recovery and publishes the branch retirement on the existing miss schedule.\n\nThe routed recovery path prompted three supporting changes. Ordinary EX branches compare bytes in parallel. An older MEM redirect clears ID/EX validity directly, so a squashed divide can hold the younger operand registers without registering a valid instruction or starting the divider. Fetch invalidates queued packets with the empty flag and forms both saturating predictor updates in parallel before selecting the resolved direction.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed. All six cocotb suites passed, including 73 pipeline cases. The directed additions cover all byte and halfword lanes, sign and zero extension, prefix boundaries, and signed and unsigned word ordering.\n- The unchanged single-channel RVFI formal gate passed all 53 checks on the selected RTL, including liveness, both PC continuity checks, and register consistency. The run required no retries.\n- Co-simulation passed the selftest and three random \n…[2988 of 6060 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:980115dbd36a8792]…\nero failures, using `wrapper_si.sv`, `checks_si.cfg`, and ALTOPS. The final divider produces the same preprocessed ALTOPS source as the staged formal run.\n- Scratch SAT proofs passed for issue capture and leading-nibble division, all seven widened odd products, and candidate residuals and fits for digits 1 through 15. The recurrence proof used arbitrary 32-bit trials and divisors accepted through the issue handshake.\n\n## Gowin measurements\n\n| Placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 100.050 MHz |\n| 1 | 104.028 MHz |\n| 2 | 104.028 MHz |\n\n`make fpga TARGET=bench` measured a 104.028 MHz median and 284.38 CoreMark iterations/s. That is 4.61% above the 99.444 MHz champion at the same cycle count and about 0.007 MHz above the replacement threshold. The median fell 0.401 MHz below the prototype. The median placement was limited by the path from the registered EX ALU result to the EX/MEM trap control, with remainder feedback 0.003 ns behind in data delay. The design used 5,326 logic cells and 1,824 registers.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
