{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/CORE_PHILOSOPHY.md && rg -n 'queue|redirect|retirement|flow' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"2:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n4:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +17.06%): With a combinational instruction response, driving `io_imemAddr` and the IF/ID PC tag from the resolved EX target removed redirect bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.9%, with the PC advancing to target plus four on acceptance and saving the target for retry when either bus blocked acceptance.\n5:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +24.76%): Registering multiply partial products and divide magnitudes in a separate EX/MEM lane and clearing ID/EX validity on redirects shortened the data-memory address and hazard feedback paths enough to outweigh a one-cycle interlock for MEM/WB forwarding when either raw source field matched the late destination.\n14:- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r6s0 (regression, +5.12%): Same-cycle EX redirect with a deferred IF increment saved 7.88% of stalled CoreMark cycles, and the divider-result path capped median Gowin Fmax at 85.247 MHz with EX-to-IF-to-ID feedback nearly as slow, leaving split-PC direct-transfer prediction ahead at 215.09 iterations/s.\n15:- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r6s1 (regression, +6.80%): The direct-only redirect variant with registered JALR, byte-parallel branch comparisons and a redirect-independent fetch register enable added 13,102 timed CoreMark cycles and gained 1.634 MHz of median Gowin Fmax over same-cycle JALR, with EX-to-decode timing tying divider issue for worst slack and leaving split-PC prediction ahead on throughput.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
