{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")]; print(json.dumps(rows[-1],indent=2))'\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261123-003-r9s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Train loop replay on the resolved instruction path\",\n  \"category\": \"predictor\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The champion takes 3,965,757 stalled CoreMark marker cycles at 97.408 MHz.\\nThe previous 16-word predictor saved 4.57% of cycles by replaying contiguous\\nloops. Recording an ordered path can also replay bodies with taken internal\\nbranches and direct jumps. A scratch fetch-trained ordered replay ran in\\n3,773,629 cycles with all canonical CRCs, but routed at 83.472 MHz in its\\nbest read-ahead variant. Its worst paths included trace capture and queue\\ncontrols. Moving trace learning to the resolved stream gives it time to\\nidentify the loop without extending the live fetch and prediction feedback.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The champion takes 3,965,757 stalled CoreMark marker cycles at 97.408 MHz.\\nThe previous 16-word predictor saved 4.57% of cycles by replaying contiguous\\nloops. Recording an ordered path can also replay bodies with taken internal\\nbranches and direct jumps. A scratch fetch-trained ordered replay ran in\\n3,773,629 cycles with all canonical CRCs, but routed at 83.472 MHz in its\\nbest read-ahead variant. Its worst paths included trace capture and queue\\ncontrols. Moving trace learning to the resolved stream gives it time to\\nidentify the loop without extending the live fetch and prediction feedback.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Tap a registered, single-packet stream of advancing EX instructions. Send\\nthe aligned source PC, instruction, resolved direction, and a traceability\\nflag. Emit one packet per instruction that enters EX/MEM valid, including\\none arrival when a divide completes. Use the resolved direction bit even\\nfor a direct branch whose target equals PC+4. Capture metadata separately\\nfrom the existing EX recovery select. The trace trainer can derive the\\nclosing branch target from its registered source and instruction using\\npage-local arithmetic.\\n\\nKeep a 32-packet circular history of the contiguous portion of that\\nresolved instructi\n…[8880 of 11952 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:32e0677f64723561]…\nbounded register check passed independently with ABC bmc3. The forward PC check had no result or counterexample within the implementation window.\\n\\n## Gowin timing\\n\\n| Placement | Fmax |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 0 | 89.198 MHz |\\n| 1 | 94.621 MHz |\\n| 2 | 94.621 MHz |\\n\\nThe 94.621 MHz median yields 249.64 iterations/s. The cycle count meets the 3.80 million target. The routed design falls short of both 98.0 MHz and the 256.92 iterations/s replacement threshold. The limiting path runs from the registered recovery address through direction prediction to the fetch queue.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-23T03:39:28.575134Z\",\n  \"slot\": 2,\n  \"round_id\": 9,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r9s2 (regression, +1.64%): Training ordered loop replay from registered EX packets cut stalled CoreMark to 3,790,292 cycles, and the same-cycle path from the registered recovery address through direction prediction to the fetch queue capped median Gowin Fmax at 94.621 MHz, keeping throughput below the replacement threshold.\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
