ode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, \n…[283998 of 287070 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:86f1a0b9d3e5e612]…\nsidual and fit equations for every trial and divisor multiple.\\n\\nGowin mapped the two banks and history to three block RAMs. Placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 85.711, 96.875, and 96.875 MHz. The median yields 264.83 CoreMark iterations per second, below the 270.715 replacement threshold. In the median placement the worst path runs between fetch queue head bits through decode and interlock logic.\\n\\nThe final single-retire run passed 52 of 53 checks within the local time budget, including the forward-PC check that exposed the original payload issue. The register consistency check was still running.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-23T06:05:06.411201Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r11s2 (regression, +2.33%): Two-bank BRAM loop replay cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.20%, and with a one-bit queue flush followed by registered correction, the fetch queue head path through decode and interlocks capped median Gowin Fmax at 96.875 MHz, below the 99.029 MHz replacement target.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
