{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,110p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r4s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Resolve execute operand selection in decode\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The dedicated address lane in round 3 moved the reported worst path to the\n  integer EX/MEM result register in all three Gowin placements. Option 0\n  reports 21.174 ns and 14 cells through ID, EX, and the ALU. EX currently\n  resolves both register dependencies, selects the forwarded register values,\n  and then selects PC or immediate operands before doing integer arithmetic.\n  Resolving operand selection on the ID side of the existing register boundary\n  can shorten EX without adding issue bubbles or a stage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compare both raw source fields of the instruction in ID against the writers\n  currently in EX and MEM. For each source, register three mutually exclusive\n  enables on ID/EX advance. Select EX/MEM on an eligible EX match, MEM/WB on\n  an eligible MEM match alone, and the local value when neither matches. EX\n  eligibility requires a valid fast result and the trap-adjusted register\n  write control. MEM eligibility uses the existing next MEM/WB reg_write\n  expression, so a misaligned load cannot forward. Exclude x0 from both\n  matches. Keep the write-first register-file path for the producer already\n  in WB and the interlock for late EX producers, including dependencies on\n  the raw source fields reported by RVFI.\n\n  For integer ALU operands, register the local A and B values with PC and\n  immediate selection already applied. Qualify each ALU bypass enable in ID\n  by whether that ALU operand actually uses the register. In EX, combine the\n  registered local value and the two existing registered forward values with\n  a single one-hot mux per operand. Keep raw register selection for the branch\n  comparator, address and JALR base, store data, RV32M operands, and RVFI.\n  This preserves back-to-back fast results while removing EX register-number\n  matching and cascaded ALU input muxes from the integer data path.\n\n  Apply the ID/EX hold to the registered enables and local operands together.\n…[1747 of 4819 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:023e07008020df9e]…\nand MEM producer destinations.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture lookahead enables and preselected local ALU operands with ID/EX. Gate the ALU forwarding enables for PC and immediate operands.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose the next eligible fast producer and replace EX operand selection with the registered one-hot muxes. Preserve the dedicated address and RV32M result lanes.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose the exact next MEM write eligibility for lookahead matching, sharing the existing alignment and write control.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the lookahead producer controls and ID source fields to forwarding and feed the registered enables to EX. Preserve the memory and RVFI interfaces.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Exercise simultaneous writer matches, trap-suppressed producer fallback, immediate and PC ALU operands, and a consumer held through a dmem stall. Check the original RVFI and latency expectations.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
