ding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-brac\n…[34871 of 37943 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d70b63d0a9113589]…\n-retire ALTOPS formal gate passed all 53 checks on its first run.\\n- All six cocotb suites passed, including the five directed redirect cases.\\n- Co-simulation passed for selftest and all three randomized traces with and without both bus stalls. CoreMark passed its canonical CRC and MMIO marker checks with both stalls enabled.\\n- CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 4,136,096 marker-bracketed cycles. Gowin measured 48.164, 49.116, and 49.116 MHz across the three placement options. The median yields 118.75 CoreMark iterations per second, 17.1% above the stated champion.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T22:24:44.478805Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +17.06%): With a combinational instruction response, driving `io_imemAddr` and the IF/ID PC tag from the resolved EX target removed redirect bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.9%, with the PC advancing to target plus four on acceptance and saving the target for retry when either bus blocked acceptance.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
