{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n --hidden -g '\"'!*.last'\"' -g '\"'!**/.git/**'\"' -g '\"'!**/.codex-home/**'\"' 'hyp-20261122-003-r1s2|536\\\\.14|736\\\\.96|7\\\\.67|13\\\\.05' .\"", "aggregated_output": "./cores/bench/LESSONS.md:1:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +536.14%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the EX address path into a shared restoring divider raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 34.631 MHz, and the seven extra cycles per operation left the CoreMark cycle count unchanged because all divides retired outside the timed interval.\n./cores/bench/LESSONS.md:2:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n./cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Fold the EX divider into six registered iterations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired DIVUs were outside it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired D\n…[18246 of 21318 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:be8af1bd218618bc]…\nh` passed five ELFs. Selftest and three random traces matched the ISS with ready and stalled buses. CoreMark passed CRC and UART validation with the prescribed stalls.\\n- The baseline and candidate both took 4,491,485 cycles between the CoreMark markers with the prescribed backpressure. `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` reported routed Gowin frequencies of 46.428, 45.562, and 45.562 MHz, a median of 45.562 MHz, and fitness of 101.44 CoreMark iterations per second.\\n\\nThe final divider step and sign correction came within 0.002 ns of the worst reported path in placement option 0. Divides add eight cycles each in workloads that execute them.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T22:02:32.500733Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
