{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i 'replacement threshold|champion|regression' cores/bench -g '\"'!LESSONS.md'\"' -g '\"'!*.vcd'\"' -g '\"'!*.hex'\"' -g '\"'!*.mem'\"' -g '\"'!*.lock'\"'\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Fold the EX divider into six registered iterations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired DIVUs were outside it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired DIVUs were outside it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU with one unsigned restoring divider in EX.\\nOn launch, capture the forwarded operands, take unsigned magnitudes for signed\\noperations, and save the result kind and signs. Run six radix-2 steps per\\nclock for six clocks using a 36-bit dividend stream with four leading zeros.\\nEach step shifts in a dividend bit, takes a 33-bit trial remainder through a\\nborrow-producing subtraction of the divisor, and shifts the quotient bit into\\nthe vacated low end of the stream. Select and sign-correct the quotient or\\nremainder from registered state. Return all ones for a zero-divisor quotient\\nand the original\n…[104777 of 107849 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:40581f6d0275158f]…\n markers, reported all canonical CRCs, and printed `Correct operation validated.`\\n- Stalled CoreMark took 4,235,641 marker-to-marker cycles, down 6.45% from the champion. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 68.723, 67.414, and 67.414 MHz. The 67.414 MHz median gives 159.16 iterations/s, up 5.47% from 150.90 iterations/s.\\n\\nThe cycle count matches the prototype. Local option 0 used 3,138 logic LUTs and 847 registers. The median is 0.558 MHz above the stated 66.856 MHz replacement threshold. The slowest local setup path in all three placements ended in the EX/MEM ALU result register.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T23:11:22.137775Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +5.47%): Page-local branch and JAL prediction with registered recovery cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45%, and the critical path into the EX/MEM ALU result register limited median Gowin Fmax to 67.414 MHz, below the 72 MHz needed to match the design with decode-stage operand selection.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
