{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"if test -f cores/bench/LESSONS.md; then cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; fi; rg -n --hidden -g '\"'!.git'\"' -g '\"'!*.vcd'\"' 'hyp-20261123-001-r6s0|204\\\\.38|Fetch resolved targets with a deferred PC increment' cores/bench\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +536.14%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the EX address path into a shared restoring divider raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 34.631 MHz, and the seven extra cycles per operation left the CoreMark cycle count unchanged because all divides retired outside the timed interval.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.67%): A 64-entry bimodal table and early JAL target selection in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, with the combinational DIV/REM path limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.47 MHz and throughput to 13.05 iterations/s versus 101.44 for the multicycle-divider winner.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +17.06%): With a combinational instruction response, driving `io_imemAddr` and the IF/ID PC tag from the resolved EX target removed redirect bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.9%, with the PC advancing to target plus four on acceptance and saving the target for retry when either bus blocked acceptance.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +24.76%): Registering multiply partial products and divide magnitudes in a separate EX/MEM lane and clearing ID/EX validity on redirects shortened the data-memory address and hazard feedback paths enough to outweigh a one-cycle interlock for MEM/WB forwarding when either raw source field matched the late destination.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +14.05%): Predicting branches and JAL in IF with a shared target and one-bit EX recovery cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, and the path from forwarding through the EX ALU to the data-memory address remained critical, leaving 48.479 MHz median Fmax behind the 57.304 MHz pipelined EX design th\n…[1457 of 4529 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:926908735930a1d0]…\n-23 hyp-20261123-001-r5s0 (regression, +12.59%): Parallel radix-16 trial subtractions removed the serial radix-4 remainder dependency and shifted the timing limit to EX-to-ID operand-selection feedback at 86.637 MHz, leaving the variant that registered full-width divisor multiples to win at 88.028 MHz with the same timed CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r5s1 (improvement, +14.40%): The zero initial remainder lets narrow restoring logic consume the leading nibble while odd divisor multiples are registered, fitting the parallel radix-16 stage into the existing nine-cycle EX schedule and raising median Gowin Fmax from 76.948 to 88.028 MHz.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r5s2 (regression, +11.90%): Registering the normal fetch PC select alongside the BTB recovery pulses shifted the Gowin timing limit from fetch feedback to the divider result path, where the 80.290 MHz median fell short of the 82.08 MHz needed for the 6.76% CoreMark cycle reduction to match the parallel radix-16 divider variant.\n", "exit_code": 1, "status": "failed"}}
