{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    if 'hyp-20261123-002-r15s1' in line or 'hyp-20261123-002-r14s1' in line:\n        entry = json.loads(line)\n        print(json.dumps(entry, indent=2)[:27000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261123-002-r14s1\",\n  \"title\": \"Split replay instruction storage from fetch steering\",\n  \"category\": \"structural\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 291.33 CoreMark iterations/s at a 98.445 MHz\\nGowin median and 3,379,213 timed cycles. The median placements report\\na replay RAM-to-fetch-PC limit. The replay table currently reads the\\ninstruction, full PC tag, and target from a single wide row in each\\nparity bank. A scratch direct replay-to-decode bypass saved only\\n22,536 cycles and reduced the median to 78.982 MHz through the register\\nfile read. The fetch steering path can be shortened at the replay\\nboundary while the instruction payload continues into the queue.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 291.33 CoreMark iterations/s at a 98.445 MHz\\nGowin median and 3,379,213 timed cycles. The median placements report\\na replay RAM-to-fetch-PC limit. The replay table currently reads the\\ninstruction, full PC tag, and target from a single wide row in each\\nparity bank. A scratch direct replay-to-decode bypass saved only\\n22,536 cycles and reduced the median to 78.982 MHz through the register\\nfile read. The fetch steering path can be shortened at the replay\\nboundary while the instruction payload continues into the queue.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"In each of the even and odd replay banks, give the instruction\\npayload and fetch steering independent synchronous read lanes. The\\npayload lane stores the 30 instruction bits in a 256-row block RAM.\\nThe steering lane stores PC[31:11], the 10-bit direct target word,\\nthe cold branch direction bit, and the local branch and JAL flags.\\nEach read lane uses the same row address, write enables, and collision\\nexclusion. Register the eight-bit row address actually read. Check the\\nstored high tag and the sampled row address against the current PC in\\nparallel, with parity supplied by the bank, so a jump or correction\\ncannot turn a coincident row address into a false hit. Keep the control\\nand payload updates atomic and clear\n…[5746 of 8818 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:93c6abcf58b0601b]…\nMHz threshold. At the measured cycle count it gives **312.39 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **7.23%**. The timed wrapper used 6,161 logic cells, 2,160 registers, 72 distributed RAM cells, and eight block RAMs. Replay accounted for 32 distributed RAM cells and four block RAMs.\\n\\nGowin repacked the inferred payload and steering address arrays into two block RAM primitives per parity, so the physical block boundaries differ from the RTL arrays. The direction and transfer flags retained fabric register outputs. Fully distributed steering and a mapping with separate physical payload blocks both produced lower medians.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-23T08:58:05.902519Z\",\n  \"slot\": 1,\n  \"round_id\": 14,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r14s1 (improvement, +7.23%): Separating replay payload from PC steering, with fabric registers for cold direction and transfer flags and parallel high-tag and sampled-row checks, lifted median Gowin Fmax from 98.445 to 105.563 MHz at the same 3,379,213 stalled CoreMark cycles.\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
