{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')\nrows = [json.loads(line) for line in p.read_text().splitlines()]\nprint(len(rows))\nfor row in rows[-4:]:\n print({k: (v if not isinstance(v, (dict, list)) else list(v)[:20]) for k, v in row.items()})\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "42\n{'id': 'hyp-20261123-002-r13s1', 'title': 'Pipeline branch recovery addresses alongside direction resolution', 'category': 'structural', 'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 271.85 CoreMark iterations/s at 99.444 MHz\\nover 3,658,045 marker cycles. Its median Gowin placement is limited\\nby normal fetch prediction feeding ID/EX recovery controls. A scratch\\nsplit that registers the two recovery addresses independently cut\\nthat path and reached 101.384 MHz median with the same validated cycle\\ncount. The limiting path then ran from the data BRAM through load\\nformatting to an EX/MEM branch comparison bit. Both paths arise from\\ncombining address or operand selection with branch resolution too early.\\n', 'hypothesis': ['motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes'], 'expected_impact': ['fitness_delta_pct', 'confidence'], 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'description': 'Carry one direction bit in ID/EX and define the registered raw-lane relations and load descriptor used by the deferred branch.'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture the fetch direction bit directly and decode ordinary branch eligibility and comparison operation in parallel.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': \"Export the independent target, fall-through and recovery selector. Register raw-byte candidate relations and the older load's descriptor when a deferred branch advances.\"}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Resolve deferred branches from the registered lane relations, preserving trap fallback, RVFI operand repair, and publication on a miss.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Capture recovery addresses and choice in separate registers, hold them for an older MEM miss, and stage the in-order predictor training packet. Connect the raw-load comparison inputs and keep registered correction priority.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Delay the aliased lookup by one cycle to keep the predictor read-before-write check on the counter update edge. Cover direct, indir\n…[19876 of 22948 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:497e63b18c115c3b]…\ne 102.98 MHz threshold. At the measured cycle count it gives **312.39 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **7.23%**. The timed wrapper used 6,161 logic cells, 2,160 registers, 72 distributed RAM cells, and eight block RAMs. Replay accounted for 32 distributed RAM cells and four block RAMs.\\n\\nGowin repacked the inferred payload and steering address arrays into two block RAM primitives per parity, so the physical block boundaries differ from the RTL arrays. The direction and transfer flags retained fabric register outputs. Fully distributed steering and a mapping with separate physical payload blocks both produced lower medians.\\n', 'timestamp': '2026-11-23T08:58:05.902519Z', 'slot': 1, 'round_id': 14, 'lesson': '- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r14s1 (improvement, +7.23%): Separating replay payload from PC steering, with fabric registers for cold direction and transfer flags and parallel high-tag and sampled-row checks, lifted median Gowin Fmax from 98.445 to 105.563 MHz at the same 3,379,213 stalled CoreMark cycles.'}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
