{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r3s0.yaml && git diff --exit-code && git diff --cached --exit-code'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-001-r3s0\ntitle: \"Generate load and store addresses outside the execute ALU\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 126.56 iterations/s at a median Gowin Fmax of\n  57.304 MHz over 4,527,760 timed CoreMark cycles. Its reported 22.15 ns\n  path runs from the register file through ID and the execute ALU to data\n  memory. Loads and stores always add a forwarded rs1 to a signed 12-bit\n  displacement. Their address currently passes through the general ALU\n  operand and result selection and the jump-link result mux.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute the effective byte address in EX with a dedicated 32-bit add\n  of the already-forwarded rs1 and the existing sign-extended I/S\n  immediate, modulo 2^32. Capture it in its own field at the existing\n  EX/MEM boundary. This removes the ALU operand muxes, ALU opcode and\n  result mux, and jump-link selection from the address path. The single\n  addition lets Gowin infer the carry chain.\n\n  Use the new field for the data-bus address, lane masks, load extraction,\n  misalignment detection, and aligned RVFI address. Capture and hold it\n  with the existing EX/MEM payload so a stalled request keeps its address,\n  strobes, and write data. Preserve all forwarding priorities, the\n  load-use and RV32M interlocks, and single-channel retirement. Drive the\n  address directly even when both request strobes are low so the address\n  path remains independent of instruction-result selection.\n\n  A scratch bench prototype routed at 62.973, 68.323, and 68.323 MHz\n  across the three Gowin placement options, using 2,860 LUT4s and 676\n  registers. The worst setup paths end at the integer EX/MEM result\n  register. With both bus stalls enabled, the prototype passed the\n  canonical CoreMark CRC and marker checks in 4,527,760 timed cycles,\n  matching the champion. The 68.323 MHz median yields 150.90 iterations/s,\n  19.23% above the champion. The acceptance margin at this cycle count\n  requires a median above about 59.94 MHz.\n\n  Verify forwarded load/store bases from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, offsets 2047\n  and -2048 crossing a 4 KiB boundary in both directions, byte and halfword\n  lanes, misalignment traps, and address and strobe stability across a\n  dmem stall. The prototype passed all six cocotb suites including a\n  directed case for these conditions and 32-bit address wraparound. It\n  passed RVFI cosim for selftest and three randomized programs with and\n  without stalls. Compare marker-window cycles and canonical CRCs, and\n  confirm in the routed timing report that the data-address path bypasses\n  the ALU.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 19\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a full-width effective byte address to the EX/MEM payload.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add forwarded rs1 to the sign-extended I/S immediate with a single 32-bit addition. Capture the address on the existing EX/MEM advance and hold it with the other request fields under backpressure.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the dedicated address for all LSU address consumers and RVFI alignment. Keep the existing ALU result on the integer and link writeback paths.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover signed offsets across a 4 KiB boundary, 32-bit address wraparound, both forwarding ages, subword lane selection, misalignment, and stable stalled load/store requests.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
