{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -in 'micro_opt|carry.select|one.hot|u_alu|bypass|critical.path' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-af44a5c\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12312, \"ff\": 460, \"seeds\": [5.14, 5.442, 5.442], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.542, \"arrival_ns\": 199.837, \"logic_cells\": 156, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 150, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T21:48:11.639001Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Fold the EX divider into six registered iterations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-bracketed cycles with canonical CRCs and retired zero divide or\\nremainder instructions inside the bracket. Both retired DIVUs were outside it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline scores 12.12 at 5.442 MHz. Its reported 199.837 ns,\\n156-cell path runs through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory\\naddress cone. The ALU places signed and unsigned division and remainder\\noperators on the same result path as ordinary address arithmetic. A shared\\niterative divider should remove most of that depth and reduce LUT use. A\\nbaseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced 4491485\\nmarker-\n…[79158 of 82230 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1600eaae9a104279]…\nI traces on ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRC and marker checks at 4,251,388 marker cycles, 6.1% fewer than the champion.\\n- Gowin placements reported 58.051, 59.747 and 59.747 MHz. The 59.747 MHz median exceeds the 56.281 MHz replacement threshold. The scored throughput was 140.54 iterations/s, up 11.0% over the champion. The hypothesis estimated 12%.\\n- Gowin reported 3,422 logic cells and 746 registers. The IF table inferred 32 distributed RAM macros. The worst placed paths end in dmem address logic, with recovery to the fetch PC close behind.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T22:47:39.260003Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (regression, +11.05%): A tagged last-successor table in distributed RAM steered fetch from the registered PC and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.1%, while the dmem address path still limited median Gowin Fmax to 59.747 MHz, below the 64.15 MHz needed at that cycle count to match the dedicated EX/MEM address lane.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
