{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if 'r3s0' in r['id'] or 'r3s1' in r['id']:\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r['implementation_notes'],sep='\\\\n')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r3s0\nGenerate load and store addresses outside the execute ALU\nCompute the effective byte address in EX with a dedicated 32-bit add\nof the already-forwarded rs1 and the existing sign-extended I/S\nimmediate, modulo 2^32. Capture it in its own field at the existing\nEX/MEM boundary. This removes the ALU operand muxes, ALU opcode and\nresult mux, and jump-link selection from the address path. The single\naddition lets Gowin infer the carry chain.\n\nUse the new field for the data-bus address, lane masks, load extraction,\nmisalignment detection, and aligned RVFI address. Capture and hold it\nwith the existing EX/MEM payload so a stalled request keeps its address,\nstrobes, and write data. Preserve all forwarding priorities, the\nload-use and RV32M interlocks, and single-channel retirement. Drive the\naddress directly even when both request strobes are low so the address\npath remains independent of instruction-result selection.\n\nA scratch bench prototype routed at 62.973, 68.323, and 68.323 MHz\nacross the three Gowin placement options, using 2,860 LUT4s and 676\nregisters. The worst setup path in each placement ends at the integer\nEX/MEM result register. With both bus stalls enabled, the prototype\npassed the canonical CoreMark CRC and marker checks in 4,527,760 timed\ncycles, matching the champion. The 68.323 MHz median yields 150.90\niterations/s, 19.23% above the champion. The acceptance margin at this\ncycle count requires a median above about 59.94 MHz.\n\nVerify forwarded load/store bases from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, offsets 2047\nand -2048 crossing a 4 KiB boundary in both directions, byte and halfword\nlanes, misalignment traps, and address and strobe stability across a\ndmem stall. The prototype passed all six cocotb suites including a\ndirected case for these conditions and 32-bit address wraparound. All\n53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks passed. RVFI cosim passed for\nselftest and three randomized programs with and without stalls. Compare\nmarker-window cycles and canonical CRCs, and confirm in the routed timing\nr\n…[2929 of 6001 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6e299445ce0e468b]…\nress, store data and request controls stable\nacross data-bus backpressure, and complete the transaction once when\nready. Keep the load-use interlock, divide schedule, and single-channel\nretirement timing. This uses the existing EX/MEM boundary and adds zero\nissue or retirement bubbles.\n\nA three-file scratch prototype routed at 62.973, 68.323 and 68.323 MHz in\nthe three Gowin placements and used 2,860 logic cells and 676 registers.\nThe worst reported path in each placement terminated at the registered\ninteger ALU result. The stalled CoreMark window took 4,527,760 cycles and\nmatched all canonical CRCs. The measured median and cycle count give\n150.90 iterations/s, a 19.2% gain over the champion. At that cycle count\nthe acceptance threshold is 59.94 MHz median Fmax. The prototype passed\nall 53 single-channel ALTOPS formal checks and passed RVFI cosim on\nselftest and the three random traces with ready and stalled buses.\nReproduce these checks, the cycle count and the three Gowin placements\nafter implementation.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
