{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r4s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-001-r4s0\ntitle: Select register operands with one execute bypass mux\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 150.90 iterations/s at 68.323 MHz and takes\n  4,527,760 timed CoreMark cycles. The worst setup path in the reproduced\n  placement-option-0 report starts at the register-file block RAM and\n  crosses the write-first ID bypass, EX forwarding, and the integer ALU.\n  The ID write-first mux and the EX forwarding mux can select the same four\n  age-ordered sources in parallel at the existing ID/EX boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Capture the raw register-file reads in ID/EX. At the same edge, capture\n  the current WB data once and two write-first hit bits qualified by the\n  actual WB write enable. For each raw rs field, also register the nonzero\n  tag matches against the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations, which\n  become the next cycle's EX/MEM and MEM/WB destinations. In EX, qualify\n  those registered matches with the current producer enables. The\n  EX/MEM enable still requires a valid fast register writer, including\n  resolved trap permissions. The MEM/WB enable still uses the retained\n  reg_write bit across a data-bus hold.\n\n  Generate four mutually exclusive selects per operand in age order:\n  EX/MEM fast result, MEM/WB result, captured ID-time WB data, and raw\n  register read. Select the value with a masked OR and feed the existing\n  ALU, branch, LSU, RV32M and RVFI operand paths. Keep the captures on\n  the existing ID/EX enable. A blocked memory operation holds the older\n  destination tags and WB forwarding payload. A divide uses the operands\n  captured at issue while its older producers drain. Preserve the raw rs\n  metadata, x0 handling, and existing interlock and retirement latencies.\n\n  A scratch prototype routed at 71.761, 72.519, and 72.519 MHz using\n  2,722 logic cells and 603 registers. The worst path still reaches the\n  integer result, with the ID write-first mux removed from the path.\n  At 4,527,760 cycles, the median implies 160.17 iterations/s, a 6.1% gain.\n  The same-cycle-count replacement threshold is 71.47 MHz. Recheck the\n  routed median, exact marker cycles and canonical CRCs. Exercise bypass\n  priority when multiple ages write the same rd, immediate load and RV32M\n  dependencies, misaligned control transfers, and the data-bus hold\n  that clears retirement valid while retaining WB forwarding.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add four future-producer tag matches, two write-first capture matches, and one shared captured WB value to ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture raw register reads, next-cycle producer tag matches, and the current WB data and qualified hit bits under the existing ID/EX advance condition.\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: Add a mode exposing raw reads while preserving the existing write-first default, reset semantics, and hardwired x0.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: Qualify the registered tag matches with producer eligibility and generate mutually exclusive age-prioritized selects for all four operand sources.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Select each forwarded operand with the four masked sources and use that value at every existing operand consumer.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Select raw RF reads for the core and wire producer tags, WB capture, write eligibility, and the four-way forwarding controls.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
