{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20261122-002-r1s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +536.14%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the EX address path into a shared restoring divider raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 34.631 MHz, and the seven extra cycles per operation left the CoreMark cycle count unchanged because all divides retired outside the timed interval.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.67%): A 64-entry bimodal table and early JAL target selection in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, with the combinational DIV/REM path limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.47 MHz and throughput to 13.05 iterations/s versus 101.44 for the multicycle-divider winner.\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a bounded multicycle execute unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline places at a median 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path, and the mapped path contains 156 cells. The four combinational division and remainder operators feed the same result mux as every base ALU instruction and effective address. The path depth makes this arithmetic the strongest candidate for the current frequency limit. An RV32IM reference run of the canonical 2K, ten-iteration, -O3 CoreMark build matched all expected CRCs and retired zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions among 2,922,016 instructions between the timing markers.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline places at a median 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path, and the mapped path contains 156 cells. The four combinational division and remainder operators feed the same\n…[8345 of 11417 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ec9585b21bb49ec0]…\nh` passed five ELFs. Selftest and three random traces matched the ISS with ready and stalled buses. CoreMark passed CRC and UART validation with the prescribed stalls.\\n- The baseline and candidate both took 4,491,485 cycles between the CoreMark markers with the prescribed backpressure. `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` reported routed Gowin frequencies of 46.428, 45.562, and 45.562 MHz, a median of 45.562 MHz, and fitness of 101.44 CoreMark iterations per second.\\n\\nThe final divider step and sign correction came within 0.002 ns of the worst reported path in placement option 0. Divides add eight cycles each in workloads that execute them.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T22:02:32.500733Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
