{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'DONE \\\\(PASS' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/bench-m-extension-lane/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-lanecheck/checks/liveness_ch0/logfile.txt\"; sed -n '\"'1,140p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.yaml; sed -n '1,100p' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/bench-m-extension-lane/build.log\"'", "aggregated_output": "45:SBY 23:15:07 [liveness_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nid: hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\ntitle: \"Register M-extension results separately from fast execute\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion runs the canonical stalled CoreMark window in 4,491,485 cycles at a median 45.562 MHz. In placement option 0, the worst path runs through a multiplier DSP to a data-memory address input. The divider's final two radix-4 steps and sign correction are within 0.002 ns of that path. In options 1 and 2, forwarding and redirect feed back through the instruction NOP mux and hazard comparison into ID/EX. These paths suggest splitting EX outputs by when they become ready and carrying pipeline validity separately from payload data.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give the M-extension a late-result lane across the existing EX/MEM boundary. Compute four parallel signed 17-by-17 limb products in EX and register them with the multiply operation. Zero-extend both low limbs and sign-extend each high limb from bit 31 only for an operand that is signed in that operation. In MEM, sum the products at offsets 0, 16, 16 and 32 modulo 2^64 and select the requested half. Pass through the bottom 16 bits of the low-low product. Compress the remaining 48 bits as three terms, combining the disjoint upper bits of the low-low and high-high products, then use a single carry-propagating addition. For DIV and REM, carry the raw magnitude and sign-correction flag through the same boundary and apply the conditional negation in MEM. Keep the current divide issue and eight update cycles, including zero-divisor and signed overflow handling.\n\n  Register the selected result in MEM/WB alongside the retirement metadata. Interlock an immediately following instruction when either raw instruction source field matches a nonzero late destination, as the ISS compares both RVFI source fields. After one interlock cycle the consumer takes the result from MEM/WB. Independent instructions keep the existing issue rate, and ordinary integer producers still bypass from EX/MEM. Source data-m\n…[6302 of 9374 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7422edb216ef4967]…\nsuite/share/verilator/include/vltstd -DVERILATOR=1 -DVM_COVERAGE=0 -DVM_SC=0 -DVM_TIMING=0 -DVM_TRACE=0 -DVM_TRACE_FST=0 -DVM_TRACE_VCD=0 -DVM_TRACE_SAIF=0 -faligned-new -fcf-protection=none -Wno-bool-operation -Wno-int-in-bool-context -Wno-shadow -Wno-sign-compare -Wno-subobject-linkage -Wno-tautological-compare -Wno-uninitialized -Wno-unused-but-set-parameter -Wno-unused-but-set-variable -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-unused-variable    -DNRET=1   -c -o Vcore__ALL.o Vcore__ALL.cpp\ng++    main.o verilated.o verilated_threads.o Vcore__ALL.a    -pthread -lpthread -latomic   -o cosim_sim\nrm Vcore__ALL.verilator_deplist.tmp\nmake: Leaving directory '/srv/hwebench/tmp/86dd60e1/d742024c/bench-m-extension-lane/obj_dir'\n- V e r i l a t i o n   R e p o r t: Verilator 5.047 devel rev v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6 (mod)\n- Verilator: Built from 0.776 MB sources in 17 modules, into 0.267 MB in 7 C++ files needing 0.001 MB\n- Verilator: Walltime 3.525 s (elab=0.004, cvt=0.026, bld=3.484); cpu 0.045 s on 3 threads; allocated 285.816 MB\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
