{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-700 && ls -la cores/bench && git log -6 --oneline\"","aggregated_output":"3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a bounded multicycle execute unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline places at a median 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path, and the mapped path contains 156 cells. The four combinational division and remainder operators feed the same result mux as every base ALU instruction and effective address. The path depth makes this arithmetic the strongest candidate for the current frequency limit. An RV32IM reference run of the canonical 2K, ten-iteration, -O3 CoreMark build matched all expected CRCs and retired zero DIV, DIVU, REM \n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Register M-extension results separately from fast execute\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion runs the canonical stalled CoreMark window in 4,491,485 cycles at a median 45.562 MHz. In placement option 0, the worst path runs through a multiplier DSP to a data-memory address input. The divider's final two radix-4 steps and sign correction are within 0.002 ns of that path. In options 1 and 2, forwarding and redirect feed back through the instruction NOP mux and hazard comparison into ID/EX. These paths suggest splitting EX outputs by when they become ready and carrying pipeline validity separately from payload data.\\n\", \"hypo\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Give loads and stores a dedicated registered address path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 126.56 CoreMark iterations/s at a median Gowin Fmax\\nof 57.304 MHz over 4,527,760 marker-window cycles. Its reported worst path\\nreaches the data RAM through the integer ALU even though each effective\\naddress is just the forwarded base register plus a sign-extended immediate.\\nThe general operand and result selection logic still couples the RAM\\naddress to the integer execute datapath and its bypass loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 126.56 CoreMark iterations/s at a median Gowin Fmax\\nof 57.\ntotal 48\ndrwxrwx---+ 5 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 22 23:49 .\ndrwxrwx---+ 3 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 22 23:49 ..\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup    0 Nov 22 23:49 CORE_PHILOSOPHY.md\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 2774 Nov 22 23:49 LESSONS.md\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  544 Nov 22 23:49 core.yaml\ndrwxrwx---+ 3 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 22 23:49 experiments\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 22 23:49 rtl\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 22 23:49 test\nb4f9e07 log: hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 regression\nafcdb59 log: hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 regression\n1b2be08 log: hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 improvement\nd113152 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0: Generate load and store addresses outside the execute ALU (+19.2%)\n3e78e0d log: hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 regression\ncbdbf69 log: hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 improvement\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
