{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; rg -n 'predictor|hyp-20261122-003-r1s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +536.14%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the EX address path into a shared restoring divider raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 34.631 MHz, and the seven extra cycles per operation left the CoreMark cycle count unchanged because all divides retired outside the timed interval.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.67%): A 64-entry bimodal table and early JAL target selection in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, with the combinational DIV/REM path limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.47 MHz and throughput to 13.05 iterations/s versus 101.44 for the multicycle-divider winner.\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct control transfers in fetch with a bimodal table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwarding\\nand the ALU, so the new fetch logic has timing headroom if its fanout stays\\nlocal.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwarding\\nand the ALU, so the new fetch logic has timing headroom if its fanout stays\\nlocal.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"In IF, predict decoded conditional branches with 64 two-bit saturating\\ncounters indexed \n…[7238 of 10310 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:54c5b1ffe9bf43c9]…\nand Gowin\\n\\nThe stalled CoreMark interval fell from 4,491,485 to 4,190,281 cycles, yielding 7.19% more iterations per cycle. Gowin placements measured 5.375, 5.468, and 5.468 MHz. The 5.468 MHz median and measured cycle count yield 13.05 iterations per second versus the locally reproduced baseline of 12.12, a 7.70% increase. The worst setup paths still cross forwarding and the EX ALU.\\n\\nPlacement option 0 used 12,883 logic cells and 653 registers, up 571 logic cells and 193 registers from the baseline. The logic increase exceeds the scratch estimate. All three placements fit.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T22:07:53.873104Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.67%): A 64-entry bimodal table and early JAL target selection in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, with the combinational DIV/REM path limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.47 MHz and throughput to 13.05 iterations/s versus 101.44 for the multicycle-divider winner.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
