per multiple. Retain\\nthe registered divisor as the first multiple and derive even multiples\\nfrom the registered odd multiples by wiring shifts.\\n\\nIn each of the seven remaining cycles, form a 32-bit trial by appending\\nthe next four dividend bits to a 28-bit registered remainder. Before the\\nlast update the scanned dividend prefix has at most 28 bits, which also\\nbounds its remainder. Subtract the low 32 bits of all 15 divisor\\nmultiples in parallel with 33-bit borrow detection. A multiple fits\\nwhen the subtraction has no borrow and the multiple's upper four bits\\nare zero. S\n…[42662 of 45734 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5067578f918eaf04]…\nlator lint passed with `-Wall`. All six cocotb suites passed. `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random programs with both ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark validated all canonical CRCs and the marker bracket at 3,658,045 cycles.\n\nGowin placements 0, 1, and 2 reached 94.915, 99.444, and 99.444 MHz. The median gives 271.85 CoreMark iterations/s, 5.04% above the champion. Placement 0 used 5,340 logic LUTs and six block RAMs, including the benchmark wrapper. The median placement is limited by normal fetch prediction into ID/EX recovery selection. The measured build was 0.671 MHz below the scratch median and used 862 more marker cycles.\n\nThe configured single-retire formal run (`FORMAL_WORK_ID=R12s2streamdec`) passed 52 of 53 checks within the local budget, including cover, liveness, and both PC consistency checks. Register consistency was still solving. A supplemental BTOR run proved one of its two assertions at the same bound before cutoff. No run reported a counterexample.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
