{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_66", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261123-(002-r11s1|001-r9s0|001-r11s0)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(l); print(d[\\\"id\\\"]); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"][:2000])'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r9s0\nForm the low two effective-address bits from the forwarded base and\nimmediate in EX. Compute exact byte, halfword, and word alignment and\nform the shifted byte mask. Latch the mask, alignment fault, aligned\nrequest pending bit, and qualified read and write bits alongside the\nexisting address. MEM uses the registered qualifiers with EX/MEM\nvalidity and drives the registered byte mask for a qualified store.\nKeep request address, enables and data stable throughout a hold. A\nmisaligned access produces its trap and retires without a bus request\nor a register write. Keep the existing load extraction and store byte\nreplication so the change preserves the memory and RVFI payloads.\n\nAn isolated prototype routed at 90.620, 97.937 and 97.937 MHz across\nthe scored Gowin placements, with 4,687 logic LUTs in option 0.\nAll six cocotb suites and the single-retire formal liveness check passed.\nCo-simulation passed for selftest and all three random programs with\nready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs\nand completed ten iterations in 3,965,757 timed cycles. This projects\nto 246.96 iterations/s, 0.54% above the champion. The longest path in\nthe median placements ended at the EX/MEM ALU result register. The\nmeasured prototype falls short of the 101.89 MHz replacement threshold\nat this cycle count.\n\n# Register data-memory request controls in EX\n\nEX uses the forwarded base and immediate to compute a two-bit address sum in parallel with the full effective address. The two-bit sum also supplies JALR alignment. EX/MEM captures the byte mask, alignment fault, aligned-request pending bit, and separate aligned read and write qualifiers under the existing data-bus hold. For halfwords, address bit 1 selects the lane pair and bit 0 determines whether the access faults.\n\nMEM qualifies the registered request bits with EX/MEM validity. A valid misaligned access retires with the trap flag set, zero RVFI memory masks, and writeback disabled. MEM still extracts and extends loads from the returned wor\n…[8987 of 12059 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2d5e7f8ae38a8c36]…\n retained version uses one-hot gather selects and parallel comparator operand routing. A compact gather variant measured 95.158 MHz median, with 52 of 53 formal checks passed and `reg_ch0` still solving near the local deadline. The one-hot version completed the full gate.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel checks on the first run of the retained RTL.\n- All six cocotb test runners passed. The new pipeline cases cover each legal lane, zero and all-ones extension boundaries, every branch condition, both source positions, double matches with stale register data, stalled responses, and misaligned loads.\n- Co-simulation passed selftest and all three random trace ELFs with and without bus stalls and validated the stalled CoreMark CRCs. CoreMark used 3,858,757 cycles between the markers.\n\n## Timing\n\n| RTL | Gowin option 0 | Option 1 | Option 2 | Median |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Champion | 89.262 MHz | 99.867 MHz | 99.867\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
