{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfor row in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if re.search('r13s[012]|r4s[01]|r8s1',row['id']):\n  print(row['id'], row['title']); print(row.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r4s0 Select register operands with one execute bypass mux\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r4s1 Resolve execute operand selection in decode\n\nhyp-20261123-002-r8s1 Pipeline wide divides and narrow the common recurrence\n# Implementation notes\n\nThe RV32M divider selects its schedule at issue by testing the signed or unsigned divisor against the nonzero magnitude range below `2^20`. The threshold test runs in parallel with the byte-wise input negation. The narrow schedule uses the leading-nibble setup and seven 24-bit radix-16 updates. The recurrence stores only the low 20 remainder bits and transfers the seventh update directly to EX/MEM. The even fit thresholds select one of eight pairs, and the odd threshold selects the residual within that pair.\n\nThe wide schedule seeds the remainder with the top 20 dividend bits and resolves the remaining three digits in two cycles each. It shares the low 24 bits of the trial subtractors with the narrow recurrence and computes the upper byte for either borrow. Each full candidate and its fit bit are registered before one-hot reduction. The fit bit checks every multiple bit above bit 31. Divide by zero follows the same completion schedule with a saved dividend magnitude or an all-ones quotient. The issue-to-transfer timing is shared by hardware and ALTOPS. The result and original RVFI operands hold through completion backpressure, and MEM applies the result sign.\n\nThe four-entry fetch queue now invalidates its contents by setting a single empty flag on redirect. The next queued packet starts at the current write slot. The fetch PC also checks sequential page wrap in parallel with the prediction target adder. Both changes address paths seen in the scored placements. CoreMark took the same 3,965,757 marker-window cycles.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed for hardware and ALTOPS.\n- The single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` ALTOPS gate passed all 53 checks, including ready-bus liveness.\n- The six cocotb runners passed all 91 cases. Divider coverage includes signed and unsigned\n…[9228 of 12300 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5a84f198673f0e8b]…\nsed selftest and all three random programs with ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRCs, memory bounds, and marker validation at **3,379,213** cycles, a **7.62%** reduction from the champion.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured **92.068, 98.445, and 98.445 MHz**. `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` reproduced the **98.445 MHz** median and scored **291.33 CoreMark iterations/s**, **7.16%** above the champion. Placement 0 used 5,952 logic cells and eight block RAMs including the benchmark wrapper. The slower option was limited by EX forwarding into the ALU. The median's worst reported path runs from a replay RAM output to a fetch PC register.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire checks on the final RTL. The first run hit a Yosys parser error on `$bits(replay_row_t)`. Using `$bits` on a signal of that type resolved it, and the retry passed. The formal wrapper holds both buses ready. Directed tests and stalled co-simulation exercise replay under backpressure.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
