{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_27", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261123-(001-r8s0|002-r8s1)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; [(print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"motivation\\\"],d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])) for d in (json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r8s0 The bench champion scores 229.25 CoreMark iterations/s at a median\nGowin Fmax of 90.915 MHz over 3,965,757 timed cycles. Its reported\nworst path runs from the divider quotient register to the EX/MEM\ndivide-magnitude register. Each radix-16 update gates all 16\ncandidate residual words with a decoded one-hot digit and combines\nthem with a wide OR reduction. A split-borrow prototype reduced the\ncarry depth but routed at 70.478 MHz, motivating a change to residual\nselection with the existing native subtract chains.\n Use a four-level binary mux tree to select the residual from the\nexisting candidate differences. At each node, use the fit bit of\nthe lowest candidate in the upper half as the select. Pair adjacent\nresiduals first using odd-numbered fits, then use fits 2/6/10/14,\nthen 4/12, and finally 8. The fit bits are monotone because they\ncompare the same trial against increasing multiples of an unsigned\ndivisor magnitude, with the existing widened-multiple guard. Each\nselection therefore directs the tree to the greatest fitting digit.\nThe direct fit selects remove the adjacent-fit decode from the\n32-bit residual path. Keep the existing one-hot quotient encoding\non its separate four-bit path.\n\nPreserve the nine-cycle EX schedule, leading-nibble handling,\ndirect final transfer, signed result handling, ALTOPS expressions,\nand result stability while EX/MEM is held. Validate arithmetic\nusing the existing digit-boundary, overflow, divide-by-zero, and\nheld-completion tests, and retain the single-retire formal checks.\nA SAT miter confirmed that both selectors produce the same residual\nfor every monotone fit vector and arbitrary candidate data. The\nscratch implementation passed all six cocotb suites, co-simulation,\nand the single-retire liveness check.\n\nIn a scratch implementation, Gowin routed at 90.242, 97.408, and\n97.408 MHz with 4,662 logic LUTs in option 0. The reported worst\npath moved to fetch-queue control. Stalled CoreMark passed CRC\nvalidation in 3,965,757 timed cycles, yielding 245.62 iterati\n…[1792 of 4864 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:898a2a2e990c7847]…\n the existing EX/MEM handshake.\n\nKeep request and completion timing identical in hardware and ALTOPS,\nand hold the completion payload and captured RVFI operands until EX/MEM\naccepts them. Check zero divisors, widened multiples, signed overflow,\nback-to-back operations, and a held completion. The ready-bus liveness\nbound and single-channel retirement contract still apply. Run the existing\nALTOPS liveness gate and exercise the real arithmetic with cocotb and RVFI\nco-simulation under both ready and stalled buses.\n\nAn isolated prototype routed at 89.601, 93.227 and 93.227 MHz and passed\nthe five-program co-simulation gate. The canonical CoreMark run matched\nall CRCs in 3,965,757 marker-window cycles, yielding 235.08 iterations/s\nat the measured median. The gain was 2.54%. Replacement at that cycle\ncount requires a median above 95.097 MHz. The new median timing limit\nruns from MEM/WB forwarding to fetch-queue head control, with a narrow\ndivider recurrence path close behind. Recheck both in the scored placements.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
