{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261123-001-r12s0\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "Let BEQ and BNE with word-aligned targets bypass the adjacent-load\ninterlock. Keep the one-cycle load-use interlock for BLT, BGE, BLTU, and\nBGEU and resolve them with the ordinary EX comparator. For a bypassed\nbranch, compare raw data-memory bytes with the other forwarded operand\npositioned by load width. Broadcast\nthe low byte for a byte load, repeat the low halfword for a halfword load,\nand use all four operand bytes for a word load. Use the older load's byte\noffset and width to set the inactive lanes' equality flags to true. For\nnarrow loads, check the other operand's upper bits in parallel. Once the\npayload bits match, the other operand's bit 7 or 15 determines the required\nprefix for a signed load. A zero-extending load requires a zero prefix.\nRegister the four lane flags, whether a payload match is needed, whether\nequality is still possible, and the prediction polarity for equality.\nMEM uses their reduction to select the branch outcome and detect the miss\nat the existing recovery edge, retaining the same priority and retirement\nschedule.\n\nHold the branch and comparison state during an older data stall. If the load\ntraps, bypass the lane flags and certify equality from the ordinary\nforwarded sources in EX. A double match also bypasses the flags and\ncompares equal when the load completes. For a single accepted match,\nequality requires both the lane reduction and the extension check. Preserve\nordinary formatted load writeback and use the accepted load's WB value for\nmatching RVFI operands.\n\nA scratch prototype passed 143 pipeline cases and all 53 single-retire\nALTOPS formal checks. It matched the reference on the selftest and three\nrandom traces with both ready and stalled buses. Canonical CoreMark\nvalidated in 3,858,757 marker cycles. Gowin routed the\nprototype at 100.236, 101.849, and 101.849 MHz, giving 263.94 iterations/s,\na 1.98% gain. The median timing path moved to the fetch queue head through\nthe register-file read. Replacement at this cycle count requires a median\nabove 104.462 MHz.\n # Load-to-branch \n…[1155 of 4227 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b5f7aca5a7971a6a]…\ne.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed. All six cocotb runners passed, including 246 pipeline cases covering each legal narrow lane, each word lane, extension mismatches, both source positions, double matches, load traps and stalls, and ordered-branch interlocks.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the retained RTL. The register consistency check took about 14 minutes. Yosys also proved the retained RTL equivalent to the initial equality-bypass implementation under ALTOPS.\n- Cosimulation passed for the selftest with ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark validated all canonical CRCs and completed ten iterations in 3,858,757 marker cycles.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 94.175, 99.396, and 99.396 MHz. The 99.396 MHz median gives 257.59 CoreMark iterations per second and falls below the 104.462 MHz replacement threshold. The median placement's worst path runs from the fetch queue head through the register file to the ID/EX ALU operand register.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
