{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor d in map(json.loads,Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()):\n    if d['id'].endswith(('r6s2', 'r6s1', 'r6s0', 'r5s1','r5s0')):\n        print(d['id'], d['logic_levels'], d['seeds'])\n        print(d['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',d['hypothesis']) if isinstance(d['hypothesis'],dict) else d['hypothesis'])\n        print(d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r5s0 14 [85.881, 86.637, 86.637]\nReplace the two dependent radix-4 steps with one exact radix-16 digit per\ncycle. During each of the last seven updates, append four dividend bits to\nthe partial remainder and subtract divisor multiples 1 through 15 from that\ntrial in parallel. Each subtraction produces a borrow and a 32-bit residual.\nA candidate fits if it has no borrow and its full multiple fits in 32 bits.\nThe fit flags form a monotone prefix. Use each fit and the next candidate's\nnon-fit to select the highest fitting digit and its residual with a masked\nOR. Digit zero carries the trial through. For a nonzero divisor, the previous\nremainder is below the divisor, so the next trial is below 16 times the\ndivisor. The stored remainder is bounded by the consumed dividend prefix\nand fits in 28 bits after at most seven updates. Preserve all 32 bits of the\nfinal remainder.\n\nCapture the operand magnitudes and result sign on issue. The first update\nconsumes only the top dividend nibble and uses four-bit trial subtractions,\nqualified by divisor <= floor(15 / digit). In parallel, register the low\n32 bits of the odd multiples 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 and 15 from the captured\ndivisor. Use shifts and add/subtract for these constants, with carry-save\ncompression before the final add for the three-term multiples 11 and 13.\nDerive even multiples by shifting their odd factors. Register a width-fit\nflag for each digit using divisor <= floor((2^32 - 1) / digit). The first\nupdate hides this setup and keeps it off the operand capture path.\n\nPreserve the existing issue edge, eight updates, and direct transfer on the\neighth update. Hold the final inputs until result acceptance. Retain the\nALTOPS equations and timing, original RVFI source operands, reset behavior,\nand external pipeline interfaces. A zero divisor fits every digit, producing\nan all-ones quotient and leaving the remainder magnitude equal to the dividend\nmagnitude. Retain the existing sign controls for divide by zero and signed\noverflow. Keep the liveness, RV32M a\n…[19590 of 22662 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a04b2f971cc010a7]…\n. The training test exercises folded aliases, saturation, simultaneous lookup and update, and training around stalls. The predicted JAL-to-REMU retirement gap is nine cycles.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed. All 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks passed on the first run of the final RTL.\n- All six cocotb suites passed. Co-simulation passed for self-test and three random RVFI traces in both stall modes. The stalled CoreMark run passed the canonical CRC and marker checks with no out-of-bounds access.\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 4,227,283 bracketed CoreMark cycles, down 6.64%. Gowin placement options measured 83.248, 90.926, and 90.926 MHz. The median gives 215.09 iterations/s, up 10.63% from 194.42 iterations/s. Placement option 0 used 4,574 logic LUTs, 1,162 registers, and 40 distributed RAM blocks.\n- Timing depends on placement. Option 0's longest path runs from a registered forwarding select to an IF recovery select. The median exceeds the 85.967 MHz acceptance threshold.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
