{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor d in map(json.loads,Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()):\n    if d['id'].endswith(('r2s0', 'r3s0','r4s0')):\n        h=d['hypothesis']\n        print(d['id'], h.get('hypothesis', h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 On an EX redirect, select the resolved target for both io_imemAddr\nand the IF/ID PC tag. The address selection is independent of the bus\nready signals. When the target response is ready and ID can accept\nit, latch the target instruction and set the registered PC to target\nplus four on the same edge. If acceptance is blocked, store the\ntarget as the next fetch PC. A data-bus stall still holds ID/EX,\nand an instruction-bus stall lets the redirecting instruction\nadvance while its target waits for a ready response. Compute the\nsequential PC and target increments in parallel before selecting\nthe next PC. Remove the redirect-driven IF and ID bubbles because\nthe bus presents the resolved target instruction. Keep the existing\nload-use interlock, divide hold, and EX misalignment checks.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype passed the canonical CoreMark CRC and marker\nchecks with both bus stalls enabled. Timed cycles fell from 4,491,485\nto 4,136,096, and Gowin measured 45.519, 45.562 and 45.562 MHz across\nthe three placement options. The resulting 110.16 iterations per\nsecond is 8.6% above the champion. The prototype passed all 53\nsingle-retire formal checks in ALTOPS mode, selftest and three\nrandomized traces with and without bus stalls, and all six cocotb\nsuites with the tighter jump timing assertion. At the measured\ncycle count, median Fmax must exceed about 43.89 MHz to clear the\nacceptance margin. Verify PC continuity and correct target\nconsumption when either bus stalls at a taken branch, JAL or JALR.\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r3s0 Compute the effective byte address in EX with a dedicated 32-bit add\nof the already-forwarded rs1 and the existing sign-extended I/S\nimmediate, modulo 2^32. Capture it in its own field at the existing\nEX/MEM boundary. This removes the ALU operand muxes, ALU opcode and\nresult mux, and jump-link selection from the address path. The single\naddition lets Gowin infer the carry chain.\n\nUse the new field for the data-bus address, lane masks, load extraction,\nmisalignment detection, and aligned \n…[2354 of 5426 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:45a45d344f6373ff]…\ne existing\nALU, branch, LSU, RV32M and RVFI operand paths. Keep the captures on\nthe existing ID/EX enable. A blocked memory operation holds the older\ndestination tags and WB forwarding payload. A divide uses the operands\ncaptured at issue while its older producers drain. Preserve the raw rs\nmetadata, x0 handling, and existing interlock and retirement latencies.\n\nA scratch prototype routed at 71.761, 72.519, and 72.519 MHz using\n2,722 logic cells and 603 registers. The worst path still reaches the\ninteger result, with the ID write-first mux removed from the path.\nStalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs and the champion's 4,527,760\nmarker cycles. This gives 160.17 iterations/s, a 6.1% gain. The\nreplacement threshold at this cycle count is 71.47 MHz. The prototype\npassed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks, all six cocotb suites,\nand RVFI cosim for selftest and all three random programs with ready and\nstalled buses. Recheck the routed median, marker cycles, and correctness\ngates after implementation.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
