{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r7s0.yaml; rg -n 'DONE \\\\(PASS\\\\)|Formal:' '/srv/hwebench/tmp/86dd60e1/e5d8161d/bench-r7s0-9mqm8hn6/skid2clean/formal/run_probe.log' | tail -4; rg -n 'DONE \\\\(PASS\\\\)|Formal:' '/srv/hwebench/tmp/86dd60e1/e5d8161d/bench-r7s0-9mqm8hn6/fixed2/formal/run_probe.log' | tail -4\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-001-r7s0\ntitle: \"Buffer two ready instructions while decode is blocked\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 215.09 CoreMark iterations/s at 90.926 MHz\n  over 4,227,283 marker cycles. A scratch profile counted 191,409 cycles\n  blocked by dmem, including 149,629 with imem ready. It counted another\n  187,364 late-result interlock cycles with imem ready and no dmem\n  hold. IF currently leaves those ready responses on the bus. Saving\n  them lets ID make progress during later imem stalls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give IF two response registers, head and tail, each holding the\n  instruction, its fetch PC and the predicted-taken bit used at that\n  PC. Track head validity and fullness with two bits. If head is empty,\n  a ready response bypasses storage to ID when ID can take it and\n  fills head otherwise. With a valid head, ID can consume it even\n  when imem is unready. Accept a ready bus response when there is space\n  or when the head can be consumed that cycle. Move the tail into head\n  on a pop from full and use the incoming response to refill the tail.\n  A pop with just one entry can refill head directly. When no response\n  is accepted, preserve the presented PC. Update the existing split PC\n  and page carry on bus acceptance, including when the response goes\n  into the queue.\n\n  Make queue consumption use the dmem hold, divide hold and late-result\n  interlock. The interlock compares both raw source fields of the\n  instruction selected for ID and uses its validity in place of raw\n  imem ready. Clear the two occupancy bits on reset and on the same\n  edge as either EX recovery pulse. Keep recovery out of the head and\n  tail payload enables and fetch advance. The existing ID/EX valid\n  kill and the occupancy clear override any speculative pop or push\n  on the recovery edge. The existing registered address select then\n  presents the exact corrected PC to the empty queue. Retry it if\n  imem is unready.\n\n  A scratch prototype passed canonical stalled CoreMark in 3,978,219\n  marker cycles, down 5.89%. Gowin measured 84.236, 91.209 and 91.209\n  MHz with 4,702 logic LUTs and 1,290 registers. The median gives\n  229.27 iterations/s, up 6.59%. Clearing the 4.6% replacement margin\n  with that cycle count requires a median above 89.504 MHz.\n  Verify full-buffer hold, simultaneous dequeue and enqueue,\n  and delivery from the buffer during an imem stall. Exercise both\n  recovery directions with a full queue and each bus stalled. Check\n  exact corrected-PC retries, wrong-path store suppression and\n  single-channel retirement order. Run the existing formal and cosim\n  gates and reproduce all three Gowin placements.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add the two-response fall-through buffer and separate bus acceptance from ID consumption. Clear only the two occupancy bits on either recovery pulse.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Drive fetch consumption from the decode hold and dependency signals. Qualify the late interlock with the selected IF response.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Expose the selected-response late dependency independently of EX recovery.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check buffered consumption, full-buffer backpressure and precise recovery. Update the exact fetch address sequences for the additional lookahead during data holds.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
