{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261123-00[123]-r10s[012]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; [print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in json.loads(s).items() if k in [\\\"id\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fitness\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"perturbations\\\"]},indent=2)) for s in sys.stdin]'\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261123-001-r10s0\",\n  \"fitness\": 258.81,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 99.867,\n  \"cycles\": 3858757,\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      89.262,\n      11\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      99.867,\n      11\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      99.867,\n      11\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.late_branch.byte_less_3_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -6.203,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 16.497,\n    \"logic_cells\": 11,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_mem\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"# Resolve load-dependent branches across EX and MEM\\n\\nDecode records each matching operand when a validated, word-aligned conditional branch follows a load to a nonzero register. EX compares the formatted load against the other source byte by byte and saves the equality flags, unsigned ordering flags, signs, and both successors. MEM resolves the condition and uses the load's WB value for the matching RVFI operands. A trapping load leaves the EX-forwarded operands and their comparison in effect.\\n\\nA MEM miss clears younger EX validity, divider issue, and forwarding eligibility. It also flushes decode and the fetch queue. EX prepares the address opposite the fetched prediction, and fetch holds that registered address when the older branch misses in MEM. This gives the MEM miss priority through the same address register used for EX recovery. MEM trains the existing folded direction counter once per valid, nontrapping branch. A missed branch captures its WB packet with valid clear and publishes it on the next cycle, preserving the ready-bus retirement bound when the corrected successor is a divide.\\n\\nThe selected timing implementation decodes the JAL and JALR link operands in ID so the existing ALU forms `pc + 4`. The ordinary branch comparison uses a 31-bit unsigned magnitude comparison and handles the signs separately. Ordinary branches still resolve in EX. The slowest median-placement path runs from the data BRAM through load formatting to a registered byte-less flag\n…[4984 of 8056 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dcc77d99731a7558]…\nm MEM/WB through EX recovery to the fetch queue write pointer.\\n\\nVerilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS modes. All 94 cocotb cases passed across eight suites. The directed cases cover simultaneous queue push and pop, head bypass, slot and history wrap, a held read-ahead packet, trace expiry, both replay branch outcomes, stalled correction and FENCE.I refetch after a stalled store.\\n\\nCo-simulation passed for selftest and all three random programs with ready and stalled buses. The stalled CoreMark run validated the five canonical CRCs, the success message and the cycle markers.\\n\\nThe single-retire `formal/run_all.sh` run completed 51 of 53 checks, including ready-bus liveness. The configured Bitwuzla runs for `pc_fwd_ch0` and `reg_ch0` were still solving at the local cutoff. No check produced a counterexample or required a formal fix. The same bounded register check passed separately with ABC BMC3 using the original properties, RTL and wrapper. The forward-PC property remains unconfirmed locally.\\n\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
