{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').open()]\nfor r in rows[-6:]:\n print(r.keys())\n print(r.get('id'),str(r.get('implementation_notes',''))[:1800])\n out=r.get('eval') or r.get('result') or {}\n print(str(out)[:650])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "dict_keys(['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson'])\nhyp-20261123-001-r10s0 # Resolve load-dependent branches across EX and MEM\n\nDecode records each matching operand when a validated, word-aligned conditional branch follows a load to a nonzero register. EX compares the formatted load against the other source byte by byte and saves the equality flags, unsigned ordering flags, signs, and both successors. MEM resolves the condition and uses the load's WB value for the matching RVFI operands. A trapping load leaves the EX-forwarded operands and their comparison in effect.\n\nA MEM miss clears younger EX validity, divider issue, and forwarding eligibility. It also flushes decode and the fetch queue. EX prepares the address opposite the fetched prediction, and fetch holds that registered address when the older branch misses in MEM. This gives the MEM miss priority through the same address register used for EX recovery. MEM trains the existing folded direction counter once per valid, nontrapping branch. A missed branch captures its WB packet with valid clear and publishes it on the next cycle, preserving the ready-bus retirement bound when the corrected successor is a divide.\n\nThe selected timing implementation decodes the JAL and JALR link operands in ID so the existing ALU forms `pc + 4`. The ordinary branch comparison uses a 31-bit unsigned magnitude comparison and handles the signs separately. Ordinary branches still resolve in EX. The slowest median-placement path runs from the data BRAM through load formatting to a registered byte-less flag.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed. The single-channel RVFI ports and unchanged verification wrappers were u\n…[8532 of 11604 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b44934fd39f5b31b]…\nueue. Replay advances only on enqueue and holds the bus at the last normal request.\n\nThe resolving edge forces the queue empty bit. Registered correction rebases the queue, cancels replay, suppresses discarded ID/EX work, and retries a blocked recovery request. The MEM resolver retains priority over EX. An advancing FENCE.I invalidates both banks, the installation state, and training history, and refetches its successor after older memory operations.\n\nDirected tests cover stalled and live admission, bank replacement, a deferred side exit simultaneous with a younger EX miss, correction retry, and self-modifying code through FENCE.I. They check exact accepted memory transactions, continuous RVFI order, and retirement PCs.\n\nThe initial formal run passed 52 of 53 single-retire checks. Its forward-PC counterexample showed that the packed-struct arrays had lost instruction payload bits in the Yosys model. The arrays now use plain packed vectors with explicitly driven packet outputs. The first retry exposed Yosy\n{}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
