overy cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, and the path from forwarding through the EX ALU to the data-memory address remained critical, leaving 48.479 MHz median Fmax behind the 57.304 MHz pipelined EX design th\n…[8676 of 11748 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f0b9708c1b552d67]…\n(regression, -0.47%): Restricting the raw-lane load bypass to aligned BEQ/BNE removed per-byte ordering logic and kept stalled CoreMark at 3,858,757 cycles, with the fetch queue head through the register-file read to ID/EX limiting median Gowin Fmax to 99.396 MHz versus the champion’s 99.867 MHz.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r12s1 (regression, +2.98%): Gowin BRAM inference required moving the forwarded LW base mux to the request register input, and the resulting design saved 29,395 net stalled CoreMark cycles against a 59,270-cycle profile bound with median Fmax capped at 102.059 MHz by the saved operand through the MEM branch comparator into the recovery register.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r12s2 (improvement, +5.04%): Pairing a dedicated fetch-queue head with registered recovery that masks ID/EX and also EX/MEM on a MEM miss removed combinational recovery feedback from the queue, raising two-bank loop replay's median Gowin Fmax from 96.875 to 99.444 MHz at roughly 3.658 million stalled CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
