{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'r5s1|r8s0|r8s1|r1s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; ids=[\\\"r5s1\\\",\\\"r8s0\\\",\\\"r8s1\\\",\\\"r1s1\\\"]; [(print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"category\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"]),print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]),print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]),print(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"],r[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"],r[\\\"seeds\\\"])) for r in map(json.loads,sys.stdin) if r[\\\"id\\\"].split(\\\"-\\\")[-1] in ids]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 structural Move DIV and REM into a bounded multicycle execute unit\nMove DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU out of the combinational ALU into a single blocking divider attached to EX. On issue, capture the forwarded operands, operation and sign flags. Advance the unsigned quotient and remainder four dividend bits per cycle using two cascaded radix-4 digit steps. Each step uses parallel widened subtracts and their borrow bits to select the residual for the largest fitting multiple of the divisor. In the eighth update cycle, select the quotient or remainder, apply sign correction and transfer directly to EX/MEM. Preserve the specified zero-divisor and signed overflow results. Use the same issue, wait and completion schedule under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, substituting the specified operand formulas for the arithmetic so the formal gate exercises the same interlocks. The eight extra cycles leave one cycle for a preceding load-use or redirect bubble within the unchanged ten-cycle liveness window when both buses are ready.\n\nHold fetch and ID/EX until the divide result can transfer and let older memory and writeback work drain. Issue only when the older MEM transaction can advance, insert EX/MEM bubbles while waiting and transfer the saved instruction and result exactly once. Report the captured source operands in RVFI and make the completed result available to the next instruction through normal EX/MEM forwarding. Base integer and multiply instructions retain their existing stage latency. The divider adds eight cycles per dynamic divide or remainder and replaces the deep single-cycle division cone with a registered recurrence. The reference profile predicts zero direct divider stalls inside the timed window. Recheck marker-window cycles under the prescribed bus backpressure, since any divide before the start marker can shift the stall sequence. At equal measured cycles, the candidate needs a median routed Fmax above 5.69 MHz to beat the champion and approximately 8.16 MHz to realize the estimated gain.\n\n# Bench m\n…[17618 of 20690 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a66ec7f5b3d2391f]…\n-- | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 88.513 MHz | ID/EX local ALU operand to EX/MEM ALU result |\n| 1 | 96.669 MHz | Registered divisor multiple to narrow remainder feedback |\n| 2 | 96.669 MHz | Registered divisor multiple to narrow remainder feedback |\n\nThe full `make fpga TARGET=bench` run reproduced the placements and scored 243.76 CoreMark iterations/s, 6.33% above the 90.915 MHz champion at the same cycle count. The 96.669 MHz median clears the 95.097 MHz replacement threshold. Branch recovery to the queue empty flag also appears in the worst-path list for placements 1 and 2. Placement 0 has a slower EX ALU path, with fetch PC page control and narrow final transfer close behind. The wide candidate registers increase placement-0 logic and register counts to 5,474 and 1,949.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.alu_a_local_31_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_20_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.298, 'arrival_ns': 16.592, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} 96.669 [88.513, 96.669, 96.669]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
