{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'r12s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; r=json.loads(sys.stdin.readline()); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "# Implementation notes\n\nMEM registers a replay observation for each completing instruction, including the resolved direction of deferred branches. A 32-word circular history tracks page-local sequential runs and identifies a span from the displacement of a short taken backward branch. Observations continue during a copy. The registered reader advances through the captured span before the writer can wrap onto it. One synchronous replay RAM holds both 16-word banks. Installation publishes the full head PC with the final write, prefers an empty bank before round-robin replacement, and protects the active bank or the matching idle head. Each bank keeps its first packet in a register. The idle read primes the second packet and the replay cursor advances only on enqueue.\n\nThe four-entry fetch queue decodes from a dedicated registered head and keeps three rear slots. A one-entry pop can replace the head directly. With rear entries present, promotion takes the oldest rear packet while the incoming packet takes the free rear slot. The ordinary bus path retains empty-queue fall-through. The interlock reads the source fields and aligned-branch flag from the live instruction or the registered head and qualifies them with the masked EX valid.\n\nThe resolving edge registers the correction, its MEM origin, and FENCE.I invalidation. The next cycle ignores the stale queue head and cancels ID/EX work. A MEM-origin correction also masks EX/MEM before any memory request, forwarding, training, or retirement. The older MEM correction preserves the recovery address captured during the branch's EX cycle and the branch retains its saved retirement slot. The correction pulse resets the divider before the target can issue. A ready correction fetch uses a sequential prediction. A stalled correction is copied into the normal PC for retry. An advancing FENCE.I refetches its successor after older memory completes and clears replay state, including an in-flight installation.\n\n## Local validation\n\nThe directed pipeline tests cover both replay adm\n…[4609 of 7681 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:06e8fa128b340e33]…\n,657,183\nmarker cycles, 5.22% below the champion. At that count the 4.6% replacement\nmargin requires about 99.01 MHz. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2\nreached 96.769, 100.115 and 100.115 MHz. The median yields 273.75\niterations/sec, 5.77% above the champion. The median placement is\nlimited by the MEM/WB result through the EX ALU. Option 0 used 5,416\nlogic LUTs and six block RAMs, including the benchmark wrapper.\nReady and stalled RVFI co-simulation passed selftest, three random\nprograms, and a directed older-MEM-miss case with a younger store,\nJALR or divide. All six cocotb suites passed with the pipeline probes\nscheduled to exercise the intended lookup cycle. The configured\nsingle-retire formal run passed 52 of 53 checks, including liveness\nand both PC consistency checks. The register check was still solving\nat the local cutoff. Reproduce the scored median and complete the\nformal gates. Exercise simultaneous head promotion and enqueue,\nstalled correction, and a correction whose target is itself a divide.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
