nchanged because all divides retired outside the timed interval.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.67%): A 64-entry bimodal tabl\n…[11152 of 14224 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6ce3e9346a3bfb00]…\nadix-16 multiples moved the width check out of the recurrence and left 33-bit carry-chain adds plus a sign check, shifting the median Gowin timing limit to the path from the registered EX ALU result to EX/MEM trap control with only 0.007 MHz of headroom over the replacement threshold at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r13s1 (regression, +5.10%): Decoupling ID branch eligibility from predicted direction and selecting between registered target and PC+4 in the existing correction cycle cut the fetch-prediction path into ID/EX, with loop replay's fetch-queue head lookup setting the 104.515 MHz median Gowin Fmax after the accompanying ALU and divider changes.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r13s2 (improvement, +7.17%): Full-PC-tagged instructions with stored direct targets let fetch replay beyond closed loops during instruction-bus stalls, with even/odd BRAM banks reading the current and next sequential PCs in parallel to cover both hold and advance and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.62%.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
