{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -o -i '.{0,100}(profile|store buffer|interlock|synchronous register file|register-file|load.use|load-dependent|false depend|byte-parallel|jalr).{0,160}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "2:correction preserve INT_MIN divided by -1 and its zero remainder.\\n\\nTreat division as a bounded EX interlock within the existing in-order\\npipeline. Launch once the older MEM operation can advance, then hold the PC\\nand the divide in ID/EX while older work drains and \n2:ur stand-in expressions with the same\\nlaunch and completion schedule so formal still exercises the interlock and\\nforwarding within its existing depth.\\n\\nEach divide or remainder adds seven cycles. The baseline profile shows no\\ndirect cost inside the timed loop, alt\n2:FI. The ALTOPS build captures the existing stand-in expression at launch and uses the same six-tick interlock.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- Verilator lint passed with real arithmetic and with `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`.\\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all five runners a\n3:ubstituting the specified operand formulas for the arithmetic so the formal gate exercises the same interlocks. The eight extra cycles leave one cycle for a preceding load-use or redirect bubble within the unchanged ten-cycle liveness window when both buses are ready.\\\n3:ainder and replaces the deep single-cycle division cone with a registered recurrence. The reference profile predicts zero direct divider stalls inside the timed window. Recheck marker-window cycles under the prescribed bus backpressure, since any divide before the st\n3:py\", \"description\": \"Include the divider in the core build and exercise an adjacent ALU producer, a load-use dependency, back-to-back division, and a stalled older memory operation. Check dependent results, RVFI operands and order, and the ten-cycle ready-bus retireme\n3:py\", \"description\": \"Include the divider in the core build and exercise an adjacent ALU producer, a load-use dependency, back-to-back division, and a stalled older memory operation. Check dependent results, RVFI operands and order, and the ten-cycle ready-bus retireme\n3: and order, dependent results, older memory backpressure, and retirement gaps of ten cycles after a load-use or redirect bubble on ready buses.\\n-\n…[49299 of 52371 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:871453db627d4e5d]…\norms the direction mismatch and presents the direct or JALR target, PC+4, and a one-bit fall-through selector. IF uses the registered pair in the existing correction cycle. FENCE.I selects PC+4, and an older MEM miss ho\n39:sts move the aliased lookup onto the new write edge and cover both correction directions, a stalled JALR across a page boundary, raw byte and halfword sign extension, reversed and equal sources, misaligned loads, and the ten-cycle ready-bus retirement gap for a di\n40:predict their sequential successor. Train on legal\\ninstructions and skip traps, SYSTEM, fences and JALR.\\n\\nRead the registered normal fetch PC and its sequential successor in\\nparallel. Use bits [10:3] for the odd bank and add bit [2] to that index\\nfor the even\n40:erred branch resolution, so an untaken branch records its actual target. Traps, SYSTEM, fences, and JALR are excluded.\\n\\nThe banks read the registered normal PC and its sequential successor. The even bank increments the pair index when the normal PC is odd. Regis\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
