{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261123-002-r8s1|hyp-20261123-001-r13s0|hyp-20261123-002-r14s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; \nfor s in sys.stdin:\n r=json.loads(s); print(r[\\\"id\\\"], r[\\\"motivation\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"],r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")[:8500], sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-002-r8s1\nThe four-entry fetch-queue champion completes ten CoreMark iterations in\n3,965,757 marker-window cycles at 90.915 MHz median Gowin Fmax. Its reported\nworst setup path runs from the divider quotient state to the EX/MEM\nmagnitude register through the 32-bit radix-16 trial subtracts and result\nselection. The benchmark profile found no divide or remainder instructions\nbetween the timing markers. Decreasing divider path delay can therefore\nraise throughput at the same cycle count.\n\n{'motivation': 'The four-entry fetch-queue champion completes ten CoreMark iterations in\\n3,965,757 marker-window cycles at 90.915 MHz median Gowin Fmax. Its reported\\nworst setup path runs from the divider quotient state to the EX/MEM\\nmagnitude register through the 32-bit radix-16 trial subtracts and result\\nselection. The benchmark profile found no divide or remainder instructions\\nbetween the timing markers. Decreasing divider path delay can therefore\\nraise throughput at the same cycle count.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Select the restoring divider schedule at issue from the magnitude of the\\ndivisor. For a nonzero divisor below 2^20, every post-digit remainder fits\\nin 20 bits. Use the existing leading-nibble setup, then seven radix-16\\nupdates with 24-bit trial subtracts and 20-bit stored remainders. Keep the\\nfinal narrow update on the existing EX/MEM transfer edge. Compute negative\\ninput magnitudes in parallel byte slices using zero reductions of lower\\nbits for each byte's carry-in.\\n\\nA divisor of at least 2^20 exceeds the top 20 bits of every dividend.\\nSeed the remainder with those bits and the quotient with 20 leading zero\\nbits during setup as the odd divisor multiples are registered. The 12\\nremaining dividend bits need three radix-16 digits. Allocate two cycles\\nper digit. Register each tentative residual and full-width fit bit first,\\nthen perform the one-hot reduction and update the quotient and remainder\\nregisters. Check all upper multiple bits before qualifying a subtraction.\\nThe wide result is registere\n…[17487 of 20559 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:71e8134d1d6d09ec]…\nhe last check, with no counterexample reported.\n\n## Gowin timing and mapping\n\n| Placement option | Fmax | Limiting path |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 99.107 MHz | Divider setup into the remainder register |\n| 1 | 105.563 MHz | Predictor trained bit into the registered lookup index |\n| 2 | 105.563 MHz | Predictor trained bit into the registered lookup index |\n\nThe **105.563 MHz** median clears the 102.98 MHz threshold. At the measured cycle count it gives **312.39 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **7.23%**. The timed wrapper used 6,161 logic cells, 2,160 registers, 72 distributed RAM cells, and eight block RAMs. Replay accounted for 32 distributed RAM cells and four block RAMs.\n\nGowin repacked the inferred payload and steering address arrays into two block RAM primitives per parity, so the physical block boundaries differ from the RTL arrays. The direction and transfer flags retained fabric register outputs. Fully distributed steering and a mapping with separate physical payload blocks both produced lower medians.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261123-001-r14s0|hyp-20261123-003-r14s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys;\nfor s in sys.stdin:\n r=json.loads(s); print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")[:5200],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261123-001-r14s0|hyp-20261123-003-r14s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys;\nfor s in sys.stdin:\n r=json.loads(s); print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")[:5200],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r14s0\nStore a ready-to-use successor for each of the three predictor states\n(untrained, trained not taken, trained taken). During MEM training, use\nthe existing page-local and alignment checks to select either the target\nor PC+4 for each state. Store its ten-bit word offset, page-increment bit,\nsix-bit direction-table index and predicted-taken bit. The untrained\ndescriptor uses the instruction sign for a conditional branch. An eligible\nJAL always selects its target, and ordinary instructions use PC+4.\n\nForm one-hot predictor-state and parity selects independently of the RAM\noutputs. Select the replay successor directly across the two banks in\nparallel with live-bus and correction PC generation. The exact hit and\nqueue space qualify the PC write and enqueue, and the selected descriptor\ndrives the replay PC data. Preserve the two synchronous current/sequential\nread addresses, 512 rows, direction-counter update behavior, ready-bus\npriority, queue behavior, and next-PC and predicted-taken values. Store\naddress bits [31:11] in each row and register the nine low word-address\nbits actually issued to each bank alongside its read. Compare both parts\nwith the current PC to preserve an exact full-PC match after holds,\ntransfers, corrections, and index wrap. Keep the same-row collision mask\nand reset/FENCE.I invalidation. The resulting 105-bit row fits three\n36-bit RAM slices per bank.\n\nA scratch implementation preserved 3,379,213 marker cycles and passed\ncanonical CoreMark validation. Gowin placed it at 93.066, 99.831, and\n99.831 MHz, with radix-16 divider timing limiting the median placements.\nThis is 295.43 iterations/s, about 1.4% above the champion. RVFI co-simulation\nalso matched the reference for selftest and three random programs with\nboth ready and stalled buses. At the same cycle count the tournament\nrequires about 102.98 MHz for replacement. The prototype falls short of\nthat threshold.\n\n# Replay successor precomputation\n\nMEM builds three 18-bit successors from the registered direct target and fall\n…[3819 of 6891 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3e845cfe9f9a2eb9]…\ncollision. A missed, stale, or aliased target read leaves the fetch\nwaiting for a valid source. Reset and FENCE.I clear availability and\nsuppress writes to the instruction copies and the metadata. Keep\nrecovery-cycle bus behavior, prediction tracking per queued packet,\nand the existing EX and deferred MEM correction paths.\n\nThe streamlined scratch target-read design completed the bracket in\n3,361,505 cycles with all canonical CRCs. It also matched the ISS on\nselftest and three random programs with both ready and stalled buses.\nAt that cycle count, replacement requires a median above 102.44 MHz.\nThe first target-read prototype\nrouted at 92.277 MHz median. Its slowest placement was limited by a\nlate comparison feeding target preference, and its median placement\nwas limited by EX ALU routing. The proposed lookup removes that\ncomparison and a duplicate target-validity lookup. A 103 MHz median\nat the demonstrated cycle count gives 306.41 iterations/s. Measure both\nthe cycle count and the three-placement median.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
