k for six clocks using a 36-bit dividend stream with four leading zeros.\\nEach step shifts in a dividend bit, takes a 33-bit trial remainder through a\\nborrow-producing subtraction of the divisor, and shifts the quotient bit into\\nthe vacated low end of the stream. Select and sign-correct the quotient or\\nremainder from registered state. Return all on\n…[72772 of 75844 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:495c081908e209e8]…\n markers, reported all canonical CRCs, and printed `Correct operation validated.`\\n- Stalled CoreMark took 4,235,641 marker-to-marker cycles, down 6.45% from the champion. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 68.723, 67.414, and 67.414 MHz. The 67.414 MHz median gives 159.16 iterations/s, up 5.47% from 150.90 iterations/s.\\n\\nThe cycle count matches the prototype. Local option 0 used 3,138 logic LUTs and 847 registers. The median is 0.558 MHz above the stated 66.856 MHz replacement threshold. The slowest local setup path in all three placements ended in the EX/MEM ALU result register.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T23:11:22.137775Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +5.47%): Page-local branch and JAL prediction with registered recovery cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45%, and the critical path into the EX/MEM ALU result register limited median Gowin Fmax to 67.414 MHz, below the 72 MHz needed to match the design with decode-stage operand selection.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
