{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-650 && ls -la cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses\"","aggregated_output":"3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a bounded multicycle execute unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline places at a median 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path, and the mapped path contains 156 cells. The four combinational division and remainder operators feed the same result mux as every base ALU instruction and effective address. The path depth makes this arithmetic the strongest candidate for the current frequency limit. An RV32IM reference run of the canonical 2K, ten-iteration, -O3 CoreMark build matched \n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Register M-extension results separately from fast execute\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion runs the canonical stalled CoreMark window in 4,491,485 cycles at a median 45.562 MHz. In placement option 0, the worst path runs through a multiplier DSP to a data-memory address input. The divider's final two radix-4 steps and sign correction are within 0.002 ns of that path. In options 1 and 2, forwarding and redirect feed back through the instruction NOP mux and hazard comparison into ID/EX. These paths suggest splitting EX outputs by when they become ready and carrying pipelin\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Give loads and stores a dedicated registered address path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 126.56 CoreMark iterations/s at a median Gowin Fmax\\nof 57.304 MHz over 4,527,760 marker-window cycles. Its reported worst path\\nreaches the data RAM through the integer ALU even though each effective\\naddress is just the forwarded base register plus a sign-extended immediate.\\nThe general operand and result selection logic still couples the RAM\\naddress to the integer execute datapath and its bypass loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 126.56 Co\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Resolve execute operand selection in decode\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The dedicated address lane in round 3 moved the reported worst path to the\\ninteger EX/MEM result register in all three Gowin placements. Option 0\\nreports 21.174 ns and 14 cells through ID, EX, and the ALU. EX currently\\nresolves both register dependencies, selects the forwarded register values,\\nand then selects PC or immediate operands before doing integer arithmetic.\\nResolving operand selection on the ID side of the existing register boundary\\ncan shorten EX without adding issue bubbles or a stage.\\n\", \"hypo\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Replace cascaded divider steps with a parallel radix-16 stage\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion runs at a median 76.948 MHz and scores 169.95 iterations/s in\\n4,527,760 timed cycles. Its reported 72-cell setup path runs from the\\ndivider's quotient register to the EX/MEM divide magnitude. The divider\\ncurrently selects a radix-4 residual and feeds it into another full-width\\nsubtract-and-select stage in the same cycle. Earlier CoreMark traces found\\nno divide or remainder instructions inside the timed window. Shortening\\nthis path can raise the clock for every instruction.\\n\",\ntotal 16\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 23 00:41 .\ndrwxrwx---+ 3 nobody nogroup 4096 Nov 23 00:41 ..\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
