{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d[\\\"id\\\"] in {\\\"hyp-20261123-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"hyp-20261122-002-r3s1\\\"}:\n  print(d[\\\"id\\\"], d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 {'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 126.56 CoreMark iterations/s at a median Gowin Fmax\\nof 57.304 MHz over 4,527,760 marker-window cycles. Its reported worst path\\nreaches the data RAM through the integer ALU even though each effective\\naddress is just the forwarded base register plus a sign-extended immediate.\\nThe general operand and result selection logic still couples the RAM\\naddress to the integer execute datapath and its bypass loop.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Give loads and stores a dedicated path to EX/MEM. Reuse the existing\\nJALR adder that computes forwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate\\nmodulo 2^32. Register the unmasked sum as a byte address in its own\\nEX/MEM field under the existing stage advance condition. JALR continues\\nto clear bit zero on its redirect target. Drive the data-memory address\\ndirectly from the new field. The generic ALU operand muxes, ALU result\\nselector and link selection are outside this address path. The EX/MEM\\ninteger result continues to feed fast bypass and integer writeback.\\n\\nUse the registered effective address for alignment checks, load-byte\\nextraction, store masks, and RVFI aligned memory addresses. Preserve\\nfull-width wraparound, misalignment traps and side-effect suppression.\\nKeep the registered address, store data and request controls stable\\nacross data-bus backpressure, and complete the transaction once when\\nready. Keep the load-use interlock, divide schedule, and single-channel\\nretirement timing. This uses the existing EX/MEM boundary and adds zero\\nissue or retirement bubbles.\\n\\nA three-file scratch prototype routed at 62.973, 68.323 and 68.323 MHz in\\nthe three Gowin placements and used 2,860 logic cells and 676 registers.\\nThe worst reported path in each placement terminated at the registered\\ninteger ALU result. The stalled CoreMark window took 4,527,760 cycles and\\nmatched all canonical CRCs. The measured median and cycle count give\\n150.90 iterations/s, a 19.2% gain over the champion. At that cycle count\\nthe acceptance thres\n…[3752 of 6824 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f0d86b917e328d65]…\nuced\\nthe canonical CRCs and matched the champion's 4,527,760 marker cycles.\\nGowin routed the design at 84.034, 88.252 and 88.252 MHz using 4,136 LUT4s\\nand 894 registers. The worst path moved into execute-to-decode feedback in\\nall three placements.\\nThe routed median and measured cycle count yield 194.91 iterations/s, 14.7%\\nabove the champion. The replacement threshold at that cycle count is 80.49\\nMHz. Recheck both measurements after implementation.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 15, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/div_unit.sv', 'description': 'Use parallel radix-16 trial subtractions and register the divisor multiples during the narrow first update. Preserve the result handshake, sign handling, and ALTOPS behavior.'}, {'file': 'test/test_div_unit.py', 'description': 'Extend exact DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU cases around digit boundaries in the first and final updates and around the 32-bit divisor-multiple limits. Retain held completion, reset, and special-case coverage.'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
