{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d[\\\"id\\\"] in {\\\"hyp-20261122-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\\\"}:\n  for k in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"):\n   print(d.get(k))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r2s0\nThe bench champion scores 101.44 CoreMark iterations per second.\nIts IF/ID interface is combinational. A taken branch or jump in EX\ncurrently discards the sequential response and spends an edge\nloading the target into the fetch PC before requesting that target.\nThe resolved target is already available to select the instruction\naddress in that cycle.\n\n{'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 101.44 CoreMark iterations per second.\\nIts IF/ID interface is combinational. A taken branch or jump in EX\\ncurrently discards the sequential response and spends an edge\\nloading the target into the fetch PC before requesting that target.\\nThe resolved target is already available to select the instruction\\naddress in that cycle.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'On an EX redirect, select the resolved target for both io_imemAddr\\nand the IF/ID PC tag. The address selection is independent of the bus\\nready signals. When the target response is ready and ID can accept\\nit, latch the target instruction and set the registered PC to target\\nplus four on the same edge. If acceptance is blocked, store the\\ntarget as the next fetch PC. A data-bus stall still holds ID/EX,\\nand an instruction-bus stall lets the redirecting instruction\\nadvance while its target waits for a ready response. Compute the\\nsequential PC and target increments in parallel before selecting\\nthe next PC. Remove the redirect-driven IF and ID bubbles because\\nthe bus presents the resolved target instruction. Keep the existing\\nload-use interlock, divide hold, and EX misalignment checks.\\n\\nA scratch RTL prototype passed the canonical CoreMark CRC and marker\\nchecks with both bus stalls enabled. Timed cycles fell from 4,491,485\\nto 4,136,096, and Gowin measured 45.519, 45.562 and 45.562 MHz across\\nthe three placement options. The resulting 110.16 iterations per\\nsecond is 8.6% above the champion. The prototype passed all 53\\nsingle-retire formal checks in ALTOPS mode, selftest and three\\nrandomized traces with and without bus stalls, and all six cocotb\\nsuite\n…[9613 of 12685 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:72db1a5ea4493b6b]…\nte lookup, rewritten instructions including traps and a divide, simultaneous bus stalls, exactly-once training and wrong-path store suppression.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with zero warnings. All six cocotb suites passed, including 25 pipeline cases.\n- Single-retire ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the first run, including ten-cycle ready-bus liveness.\n- Co-simulation matched the self-test and three random RVFI traces on ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRC and marker checks at 4,251,388 marker cycles, 6.1% fewer than the champion.\n- Gowin placements reported 58.051, 59.747 and 59.747 MHz. The 59.747 MHz median exceeds the 56.281 MHz replacement threshold. The scored throughput was 140.54 iterations/s, up 11.0% over the champion. The hypothesis estimated 12%.\n- Gowin reported 3,422 logic cells and 746 registers. The IF table inferred 32 distributed RAM macros. The worst placed paths end in dmem address logic, with recovery to the fetch PC close behind.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
