{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_88", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r6s1.yaml <<'END_HYPOTHESIS'\nid: hyp-20261123-002-r6s1\ntitle: \\\"Fetch resolved direct targets in the execute cycle\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,527,760 marker-window cycles at a median 88.028 MHz.\n  A stalled CoreMark trace counted 294,425 taken conditional branches and\n  49,367 JALs in that window. Each currently waits for a clock edge to put\n  its resolved target on the instruction bus. Retiming the fetch increment\n  addresses the target-to-next-PC feedback of the earlier same-cycle redirect\n  attempt and can trade a small clock loss for fewer cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Drive the current instruction request and its IF/ID PC tag from the\n  resolved EX target for a valid, aligned, taken branch or JAL. Use the\n  combinational response as the next instruction when both stages can\n  advance. Keep request address selection independent of both bus-ready\n  inputs. A data-memory hold retains the older instruction and commits no\n  redirect. If EX advances while instruction memory is stalled, remember\n  the exact target for retry. JALR continues to squash the sequential\n  instruction and put its resolved target in registered fetch state.\n\n  Represent fetch state as a 30-bit word-address base plus a registered\n  advance bit. An accepted request records the request address and sets\n  advance. A redirect that needs retry, or a JALR, records the target with\n  advance clear. Apply the word increment from registered state with\n  parallel lower-bit reductions, keeping it off the EX target path. Resolve\n  conditional branches with four byte-level equality and less-than\n  comparisons and combine them from most to least significant byte. Resolve\n  the JALR alignment check from the low two operand bits and use that check\n  for link-write eligibility. Keep architectural RVFI successors, traps,\n  data-memory side effects, late-result interlocks, and single-channel\n  retirement. A ready-bus direct redirect removes one bubble and preserves\n  the ten-cycle retirement bound.\n\n  An isolated prototype matched all five co-simulation programs and\n  completed the stalled CoreMark window in 4,184,194 cycles with canonical\n  CRCs and both markers. Gowin placements measured 75.075, 85.169 and\n  85.169 MHz with 4,104 LUTs and 899 registers in option 0. The resulting\n  203.55 iter/s is 4.70% above the champion. At that cycle count the entry\n  needs above 85.091 MHz median to clear the replacement margin. The median\n  placements were limited by the existing divider result path, and the\n  margin is sensitive to routing. Reproduce the cycle count and all three\n  placements after implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Select the live direct target or the registered sequential word address for the request and its PC tag. Keep the base and advance bit across a hold, retry an unaccepted direct target, and register JALR targets.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Combine parallel byte comparisons for branch conditions and expose the resolved redirect independently of data-bus ready. Use exact low-bit alignment checks and qualify jump link writes from the jump alignment check.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Route the EX redirect and its JALR qualification to fetch. Connect the EX hold separately from fetch acceptance so the bus-ready inputs affect state updates only.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Use the JALR redirect to squash the sequential response. Let an aligned direct redirect accept the response at the target under the normal advance and readiness rules.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Check direct target acceptance and retry with either bus stalled, including an older held dmem request and a target JAL or dependent instruction. Check continuous RVFI PCs, precise misalignment traps, wrong-path store exclusion, the registered JALR redirect, and the tightened direct JAL-to-REMU gap while retaining the divide and load-use bounds.\\\"\nEND_HYPOTHESIS\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
