{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|gshare|return.address|bimodal|tournament' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-450\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct control transfers in fetch with a bimodal table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwar\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct transfers in fetch with a direction-only recovery check\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current multicycle-divider core still discards one fetch slot on each\\ntaken branch or jump that changes the PC. The previous 64-entry bimodal\\npredictor with early JAL cut the stalled CoreMark interval from 4,491,485 to\\n4,190,281 cycles. That run retained the old combinational divider, \n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Predict the next fetch with a tagged last-successor buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench champion takes 4,527,760 CoreMark marker cycles at\\n57.304 MHz. The previous bimodal predictor saved 301,204 cycles on its\\nparent and added instruction predecode and a target adder to fetch.\\nA target buffer can steer fetch from the registered PC alone and cover\\nrecurring JALR target\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Predict page-local direct transfers with registered recovery\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion runs CoreMark in 4,527,760 timed cycles at 68.323 MHz.\\nA stalled retirement trace shows that 294,417 of 294,425 nonsequential\\nconditional branches and 47,645 of 49,367 nonsequential JALs target the\\nsame 4 KiB page as their source. A small direction table and a page-local\\ntarget adder \n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Predict taken transfers with a banked target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core loses one fetch slot on a taken transfer and runs CoreMark\\nin 4,527,760 timed cycles at\n…[1438 of 4510 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:972a363984313c66]…\nr both stall sources. Its retirement trace contains about\\n1.17 million words in consecutive repeats of contiguous loops \n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Train loop replay on the resolved instruction path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion takes 3,965,757 stalled CoreMark marker cycles at 97.408 MHz.\\nThe previous 16-word predictor saved 4.57% of cycles by replaying contiguous\\nloops. Recording an ordered path can also replay bodies with taken internal\\nbranches and direct jumps. A scratch fetch-trained ordered replay ran in\\n3,773,6\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Pipeline prediction bits for loop replay in the fetch queue\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion takes 3,965,757 stalled CoreMark marker cycles at\\n97.408 MHz. The r9s2 resolved loop predictor cut that to 3,790,292\\ncycles, but the recovery PC through live direction prediction to a\\nfetch queue slot capped it at 94.621 MHz. Direct replay into decode\\nmoved the scratch designs' \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
