{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n  try: d=json.loads(s)\n  except ValueError: continue\n  if d.get('category') == 'predictor' and any(k in d['id'] for k in ('r8s','r9s','r10s','r5s','r4s')):\n    for k,v in d.items():\n      if k not in ('changes','timing_report','worst_paths','eval','result'):\n        print(k,':',v)\n    print()\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id : hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\ntitle : Predict page-local direct transfers with registered recovery\ncategory : predictor\nmotivation : The champion runs CoreMark in 4,527,760 timed cycles at 68.323 MHz.\nA stalled retirement trace shows that 294,417 of 294,425 nonsequential\nconditional branches and 47,645 of 49,367 nonsequential JALs target the\nsame 4 KiB page as their source. A small direction table and a page-local\ntarget adder can cover these transfers. Registering the recovery\naddresses and their selects separately keeps the late EX decision off\nthe fetch address and the wide next-PC mux in the resolving cycle.\n\nhypothesis : {'motivation': 'The champion runs CoreMark in 4,527,760 timed cycles at 68.323 MHz.\\nA stalled retirement trace shows that 294,417 of 294,425 nonsequential\\nconditional branches and 47,645 of 49,367 nonsequential JALs target the\\nsame 4 KiB page as their source. A small direction table and a page-local\\ntarget adder can cover these transfers. Registering the recovery\\naddresses and their selects separately keeps the late EX decision off\\nthe fetch address and the wide next-PC mux in the resolving cycle.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"In IF, predecode legal conditional branches and JAL using the fetched\\ninstruction. Predict conditional direction with 32 two-bit saturating\\ncounters indexed by PC[6:2] XOR PC[11:7]. Use a resettable validity\\nbitmap and predict backward taken and forward not taken on cold slots.\\nInitialize a 