{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-202611(22-003-r3s2|23-003-r(6s2|9s2|10s2|11s2))\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; keys=(\\\"id\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"); [print(*(str(e[k]).split(\\\"\\\\n## Timing\\\")[0][:4000] for k in keys),sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for e in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\n{'motivation': 'The current bench champion takes 4,527,760 CoreMark marker cycles at\\n57.304 MHz. The previous bimodal predictor saved 301,204 cycles on its\\nparent and added instruction predecode and a target adder to fetch.\\nA target buffer can steer fetch from the registered PC alone and cover\\nrecurring JALR targets, including stable return sites.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-successor buffer indexed by\\nPC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Restrict lookup and training to word-aligned\\ninstruction PCs below 0x00100000. Tag each entry with PC[19:8], which\\ntogether with the index identifies the full in-range source PC. Store the\\nlast resolved successor as an 18-bit word address and reconstruct the\\nbyte address with zero upper and lower bits. A valid tag hit supplies\\nthat successor and a miss supplies PC+4. Use one asynchronous read and\\none clocked write for the table payload, with a separate resettable valid\\nbitmap so the payload can infer distributed RAM. Use pre-write contents\\nfor a same-edge lookup and update. Keep io_imemAddr driven by the\\nregistered fetch PC and advance that PC on accepted fetch.\\n\\nTrain on valid advancing nontrapping conditional branches, JAL and JALR,\\nincluding not-taken conditional branches. Suppress writes with an\\nout-of-range or misaligned successor. Pipeline the write index, tag and\\nsuccessor through free-running registers with a separate reset-cleared\\nwrite-valid pulse. Commit the registered update on the following edge,\\nincluding when a new dmem hold begins. Keep the existing forwarded EX\\nbranch and JALR resolution, link writes and alignment traps. Compare the\\ncurrent registered fetch PC with both the computed control-transfer target\\nand PC+4 in parallel. Select the relevant mismatch after direction and\\ntrap resolution. Validate every advancing EX instruction so a stale\\nentry on an ordinary or illegal instruction also recovers to PC+4.\\nDrive recovery and RVFI PC reporting from the same resolved successor.\\n\\nAn older dmem hold or a\n…[31923 of 34995 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3d741f27116fe8e4]…\nker check in 3,658,038 cycles, 5.20% below the champion.\n\nTiming changes also encode the direction table as taken, not-taken, and strength planes, compare EX branch operands in byte groups, and use the full-width radix-16 trial borrow to qualify divider candidates. The predictor preserves the existing two-bit counter transitions. A combinational SAT check proved equivalence of the old and new divider candidate residual and fit equations for every trial and divisor multiple.\n\nGowin mapped the two banks and history to three block RAMs. Placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 85.711, 96.875, and 96.875 MHz. The median yields 264.83 CoreMark iterations per second, below the 270.715 replacement threshold. In the median placement the worst path runs between fetch queue head bits through decode and interlock logic.\n\nThe final single-retire run passed 52 of 53 checks within the local time budget, including the forward-PC check that exposed the original payload issue. The register consistency check was still running.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
