{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor v in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if 'r11s2' in str(v.get('id')):\n  lines=v['implementation_notes'].splitlines()\n  for i,s in enumerate(lines):\n   if 'direction table' in s or 'timing changes' in s.lower(): print('\\\\n'.join(lines[max(i-1,0):i+3]))\nPY\nrg -n 'direction' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py | tail -30\"","aggregated_output":"\nTiming changes also encode the direction table as taken, not-taken, and strength planes, compare EX branch operands in byte groups, and use the full-width radix-16 trial borrow to qualify divider candidates. The predictor preserves the existing two-bit counter transitions. A combinational SAT check proved equivalence of the old and new divider candidate residual and fit equations for every trial and divisor multiple.\n\nGowin mapped the two banks and history to three block RAMs. Placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 85.711, 96.875, and 96.875 MHz. The median yields 264.83 CoreMark iterations per second, below the 270.715 replacement threshold. In the median placement the worst path runs between fetch queue head bits through decode and interlock logic.\n860:async def direction_misses_retry_under_backpressure(dut, stall_fetch, stall_data):\n934:async def folded_direction_training_and_read_before_write(dut):\n975:            assert not int(dut.u_if.branch_direction.value)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
