{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['category']=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d['title'], d['outcome'],d['fitness'],d['cycles'],d.get('bsram'),d['fmax_mhz'])\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r13s2','r12s2','r3s2','r9s2')):\n   print(d['hypothesis'])\n   print(d['implementation_notes'][:6000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 Predict direct control transfers in fetch with a bimodal table regression 13.05 4190281 6 5.468\nhyp-20261122-003-r2s2 Predict direct transfers in fetch with a direction-only recovery check regression 115.69 4190281 6 48.479\nhyp-20261122-003-r3s2 Predict the next fetch with a tagged last-successor buffer regression 140.54 4251388 6 59.747\n{'motivation': 'The current bench champion takes 4,527,760 CoreMark marker cycles at\\n57.304 MHz. The previous bimodal predictor saved 301,204 cycles on its\\nparent and added instruction predecode and a target adder to fetch.\\nA target buffer can steer fetch from the registered PC alone and cover\\nrecurring JALR targets, including stable return sites.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-successor buffer indexed by\\nPC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Restrict lookup and training to word-aligned\\ninstruction PCs below 0x00100000. Tag each entry with PC[19:8], which\\ntogether with the index identifies the full in-range source PC. Store the\\nlast resolved successor as an 18-bit word address and reconstruct the\\nbyte address with zero upper and lower bits. A valid tag hit supplies\\nthat successor and a miss supplies PC+4. Use one asynchronous read and\\none clocked write for the table payload, with a separate resettable valid\\nbitmap so the payload can infer distributed RAM. Use pre-write contents\\nfor a same-edge lookup and update. Keep io_imemAddr driven by the\\nregistered fetch PC and advance that PC on accepted fetch.\\n\\nTrain on valid advancing nontrapping conditional branches, JAL and JALR,\\nincluding not-taken conditional branches. Suppress writes with an\\nout-of-range or misaligned successor. Pipeline the write index, tag and\\nsuccessor through free-running registers with a separate reset-cleared\\nwrite-valid pulse. Commit the registered update on the following edge,\\nincluding when a new dmem hold begins. Keep the existing forwarded EX\\nbranch and JALR resolution, link writes and alignment traps. Compare the\\ncurrent registered fetch PC with both the \n…[31310 of 34382 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6a412ac642c3b6aa]…\nsed selftest and all three random programs with ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRCs, memory bounds, and marker validation at **3,379,213** cycles, a **7.62%** reduction from the champion.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured **92.068, 98.445, and 98.445 MHz**. `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` reproduced the **98.445 MHz** median and scored **291.33 CoreMark iterations/s**, **7.16%** above the champion. Placement 0 used 5,952 logic cells and eight block RAMs including the benchmark wrapper. The slower option was limited by EX forwarding into the ALU. The median's worst reported path runs from a replay RAM output to a fetch PC register.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire checks on the final RTL. The first run hit a Yosys parser error on `$bits(replay_row_t)`. Using `$bits` on a signal of that type resolved it, and the retry passed. The formal wrapper holds both buses ready. Directed tests and stalled co-simulation exercise replay under backpressure.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
