{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r5s2','r12s1','r13s1')):\n  print(d['id'],d['cycles'],d['fmax_mhz'],d['fitness'],d['implementation_notes'][:3500])\nPY\ngit log -5 --oneline\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-003-r5s2 4221702 80.29 190.18 # Bench core banked branch target buffer\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Fetch indexes four 16-row banks with `PC[7:2]`. Each bank checks the `PC[19:8]` tag and the direction counter locally, then masks its 18-bit target word address. Fetch selects the OR of the masked targets only for an accepted, legal branch, JAL, or JALR response in the low 1 MiB.\n- EX compares the current fetch address against the target and PC+4 in parallel. A mismatch squashes the younger instruction and registers the appropriate recovery pulse. The next cycle uses the correction for both the imem request and the instruction's PC tag, and saves that address for retry when fetch is blocked. A misaligned taken transfer resumes at PC+4 with the trap set and the link write suppressed.\n- A valid, advancing control transfer with aligned, in-range source and target addresses queues one registered update. Each bank reads its old tag and direction counter at write time. Conditional branches allocate weakly taken, saturate on subsequent matches, and update the target on taken outcomes. JAL and JALR record strongly taken. Bank validity and update validity reset, and simultaneous fetches see pre-write contents.\n\n## Implementation choices\n\n- Each bank uses separate tag, direction, and target arrays. The training port reads the old tag and counter, and a separate write enable preserves the target on a not-taken outcome.\n- Fetch registers the normal PC select alongside the two recovery pulses. Prediction, sequential advance, and retry feed the PC through a one-hot selection. This shortened the fetch path that limited the first Gowin build to a 74.454 MHz median. The worst placed paths in the revised build end at the divider result registers.\n- A changed control transfer with a stale entry can incur repeated recovery when its new target is misaligned or outside the cacheable range. Each recovery uses the exact architectural successor.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All six cocotb suites p\n…[6456 of 9528 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:66ad7de6d754a4a2]…\n gate passed all 53 checks on the selected RTL, including liveness, with zero failures. Local formal used ALTOPS. The cocotb divider suite and RVFI cosimulation exercised the hardware arithmetic.\n- RVFI cosimulation passed selftest and three random programs with ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark matched all five canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,658,045 marker cycles.\n- Gowin placement options measured 97.997, 104.515, and 104.515 MHz. The median gives 285.71 iterations/s, a 5.10% gain over 271.85 iterations/s. Placement 0 remains limited by the divider recurrence. The other two placements are limited by replay head lookup into the fetch queue, with WB-to-ALU and branch correction paths close behind.\n\n19732e3 log: hyp-20261123-003-r13s2 improvement\neaa5b9f log: hyp-20261123-002-r13s1 regression\nf4f4afa log: hyp-20261123-001-r13s0 regression\n985001f hyp-20261123-003-r13s2: Predict and replay the next fetch through instruction stalls (+7.2%)\ncd074ce log: hyp-20261123-003-r12s2 improvement\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
