{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor r in rows[-3:]:\n print(r['id'], r['fitness'],r['cycles'],r['fmax_mhz'],r['lut4'],r['ff'],r['bsram'],r['lutram'])\n print(r['seeds'])\n print(r['perturbations'])\n print(r['implementation_notes'][-4400:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r14s0 276.45 3379213 93.417 6004 2208 10 40\n[96.988, 93.417, 93.417]\n[[0, 96.988, 13], [1, 93.417, 11], [2, 93.417, 11]]\n# Replay successor precomputation\n\nMEM builds three 18-bit successors from the registered direct target and fall-through. Each successor stores the word offset, the page increment, the folded predictor index, and the predicted-taken bit. Aligned page-local conditional branches use the sign for an untrained predictor entry and the counter direction for a trained entry. Page-local JAL uses its target in all three states. Other replayable instructions use PC+4.\n\nThe two synchronous RAM banks still hold 256 rows each. Each 105-bit row holds the 21-bit high tag, 30 instruction bits, and three successors. Each bank registers the nine low word-address bits actually read. Hits require a matching issued address, a matching RAM tag, and registered availability. Same-row collisions mask the read, and reset and FENCE.I clear availability and suppress writes.\n\nFetch selects successor data with one-hot bank and predictor-state controls while the live bus and correction logic compute their successors. Live and replay advancements have separate enables for page updates and queue insertion. Ready instruction-bus responses retain priority, including fall-through to decode.\n\nGowin inferred three block RAM slices per bank and used ten block RAMs in the benchmark wrapper and core combined.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint and all seven cocotb suites passed, including 56 pipeline cases. The directed tests cover both trained directions, the untrained descriptor contents, exact tags and issued indices across wrap and redirects, collision and invalidation, the page-end JAL and sequential wrap, and RVFI retirement order and PCs.\n- Co-simulation matched selftest and three random programs with ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark validated the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,379,213 marker cycles.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` passe\n…[2696 of 5768 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c4873ad6542fc91a]…\n-- |\n| 0 | 99.107 MHz | Divider setup into the remainder register |\n| 1 | 105.563 MHz | Predictor trained bit into the registered lookup index |\n| 2 | 105.563 MHz | Predictor trained bit into the registered lookup index |\n\nThe **105.563 MHz** median clears the 102.98 MHz threshold. At the measured cycle count it gives **312.39 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **7.23%**. The timed wrapper used 6,161 logic cells, 2,160 registers, 72 distributed RAM cells, and eight block RAMs. Replay accounted for 32 distributed RAM cells and four block RAMs.\n\nGowin repacked the inferred payload and steering address arrays into two block RAM primitives per parity, so the physical block boundaries differ from the RTL arrays. The direction and transfer flags retained fabric register outputs. Fully distributed steering and a mapping with separate physical payload blocks both produced lower medians.\n\nhyp-20261123-003-r14s2 296.08 3361505 99.529 6736 2165 10 136\n[94.989, 99.529, 99.529]\n[[0, 94.989, 12], [1, 99.529, 9], [2, 99.529, 9]]\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
