{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": ?\\\"predictor\\\"|r3s2|r4s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct control transfers in fetch with a bimodal table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwarding\\nand the ALU, so the new fetch logic has timing headroom if its fanout stays\\nlocal.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwarding\\nand the ALU, so the new fetch logic has timing headroom if its fanout stays\\nlocal.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"In IF, predict decoded conditional branches with 64 two-bit saturating\\ncounters indexed by pc[7:2] XOR pc[13:8]. Predict backward branches taken\\nand forward branches not taken on untrained entries. Initialize each entry\\nweakly toward its first outcome, then train once per advancing, valid,\\nnontrapping conditional branch. When prediction and training share one\\nentry in a cycle, prediction uses the pre-update state. Predict JAL taken\\nand resolve JALR in EX.\\nUse a shared PC-relative adder for B and JAL immediates and require the\\nspeculative target to be four-byte aligned and below 0x00100000. Select\\nthe predicted successor only when the corresponding fetch enters ID/EX.\\n\\nThe registered IF PC already holds the successor selected for the instruction\\nin ID/EX. During EX, compare that PC with the resolved architectural successor\\nfor valid control transfers. Redirect and kill the younger fetch on mismatch,\\nincluding predicted-taken branches that resolve not taken. Freeze fetch and EX\\ntogether during older dmem backpressure, then apply recove\n…[38506 of 41578 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2463978402791e3c]…\n markers, reported all canonical CRCs, and printed `Correct operation validated.`\\n- Stalled CoreMark took 4,235,641 marker-to-marker cycles, down 6.45% from the champion. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 68.723, 67.414, and 67.414 MHz. The 67.414 MHz median gives 159.16 iterations/s, up 5.47% from 150.90 iterations/s.\\n\\nThe cycle count matches the prototype. Local option 0 used 3,138 logic LUTs and 847 registers. The median is 0.558 MHz above the stated 66.856 MHz replacement threshold. The slowest local setup path in all three placements ended in the EX/MEM ALU result register.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T23:11:22.137775Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +5.47%): Page-local branch and JAL prediction with registered recovery cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45%, and the critical path into the EX/MEM ALU result register limited median Gowin Fmax to 67.414 MHz, below the 72 MHz needed to match the design with decode-stage operand selection.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +536.14%): Moving combinational DIV/REM off the EX address path into a shared restoring divider raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 34.631 MHz, and the seven extra cycles per operation left the CoreMark cycle count unchanged because all divides retired outside the timed interval.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +736.96%): Two cascaded radix-4 steps with parallel subtracts kept EX division to nine cycles at a median routed core Fmax of 45.562 MHz, leaving one cycle for a load-use or redirect bubble within the ten-cycle ready-bus RVFI liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.67%): A 64-entry bimodal table and early JAL target selection in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.7%, with the combinational DIV/REM path limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.47 MHz and throughput to 13.05 iterations/s versus 101.44 for the multicycle-divider winner.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +17.06%): With a combinational instruction response, driving `io_imemAddr` and the IF/ID PC tag from the resolved EX target removed redirect bubbles and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.9%, with the PC advancing to target plus four on acceptance and saving the target for retry when either bus blocked acceptance.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +24.76%): Registering multiply partial products and divide magnitudes in a separate EX/MEM lane and clearing ID/EX validity on redirects shortened the data-memory address and hazard feedback paths enough to outweigh a one-cycle interlock for MEM/WB forwarding when either raw source field matched the late destination.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +14.05%): Predicting branches and JAL in IF with a s