{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261123-003-r5s2|hyp-20261122-003-r4s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct control transfers in fetch with a bimodal table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwarding\\nand the ALU, so the new fetch logic has timing headroom if its fanout stays\\nlocal.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark core fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken transfer.\\nIts unregistered IF/ID bundle means each nonsequential transfer discards one\\nfetch slot. Predicting hot loop branches and direct jumps can reuse those\\nslots for useful work. The measured 156-cell critical path is in forwarding\\nand the ALU, so the new fetch logic has timing headroom if its fanout stays\\nlocal.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"In IF, predict decoded conditional branches with 64 two-bit saturating\\ncounters indexed by pc[7:2] XOR pc[13:8]. Predict backward branches taken\\nand forward branches not taken on untrained entries. Initialize each entry\\nweakly toward its first outcome, then train once per advancing, valid,\\nnontrapping conditional branch. When prediction and training share one\\nentry in a cycle, prediction uses the pre-update state. Predict JAL taken\\nand resolve JALR in EX.\\nUse a shared PC-relative adder for B and JAL immediates and require the\\nspeculative target to be four-byte aligned and below 0x00100000. Select\\nthe predicted successor only when the corresponding fetch enters ID/EX.\\n\\nThe registered IF PC already holds the successor selected for the instruction\\nin ID/EX. During EX, compare that PC with the resolved architectural successor\\nfor valid control transfers. Redirect and kill the younger fetch on mismatch,\\nincluding predicted-taken branches that resolve not taken. Freeze fetch and EX\\ntogether during older dmem backpressure, then apply recove\n…[50164 of 53236 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c8778bb125669742]…\n start and stop markers at 15,057 and 4,236,759, for 4,221,702 timed cycles, a 6.76% reduction.\\n- The full FPGA gate reported Gowin placements of 78.572, 80.290, and 80.290 MHz. The median is 2.480 MHz below the scratch prototype and gives 190.18 iterations/s, up 11.91% from 169.95. Option 0 used 4,138 logic LUTs, 982 registers, and 84 distributed RAM blocks across the benchmark design.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including liveness and register consistency. There were zero failed checks and no formal retries.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T23:39:41.430092Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r5s2 (regression, +11.90%): Registering the normal fetch PC select alongside the BTB recovery pulses shifted the Gowin timing limit from fetch feedback to the divider result path, where the 80.290 MHz median fell short of the 82.08 MHz needed for the 6.76% CoreMark cycle reduction to match the parallel radix-16 divider variant.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
