{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; records=list(map(json.loads,sys.stdin)); [print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for r in records[1:4]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\nPredict legal conditional branches and JAL in IF. Use 64 two-bit saturating\ncounters indexed by pc[7:2] XOR pc[13:8], with a resettable valid bitmap\nand a backward-taken, forward-not-taken default for cold entries. Use the\nprior counter state on a same-index lookup and update. Train each valid,\nadvancing, nontrapping conditional branch once, initializing a cold entry\nweakly toward its actual outcome. JAL predicts taken and JALR resolves in EX.\n\nCompute the exact full-width B/J target with a shared IF adder. Select it\nspeculatively only if it is word-aligned and below 0x00100000. Advance the\nregistered fetch PC on instruction acceptance and carry that same full\ntarget and the actual prediction select into ID/EX. This lets EX reuse the\ntarget for direct transfers and recover from a direction mismatch with a\none-bit comparison. Redirect to the resolved target or PC+4 as appropriate.\nTreat a direct target equal to PC+4 as sequential on both sides. Determine\nthe architectural outcome independently of the speculation address guard.\nJALR retains the forwarded base-plus-immediate target and bit-zero clearing.\nPreserve existing alignment traps, suppressed link writes and RVFI next-PC.\n\nGate recovery and training on valid EX advance. Hold the fetch PC and\nID/EX prediction metadata during older dmem backpressure. On advance, EX\nrecovery overrides fetch stalls. Feed imem data directly to decode and\nsquash the younger instruction by clearing only the ID/EX valid and control\nfields. An unavailable imem response must also enter EX with zero control\nso it cannot issue memory, divider or forwarding side effects. Qualify\nload-use detection with imemReady and keep the older dmem hold independent\nof redirect. Keep imemAddr driven from the registered PC. This keeps the\nEX redirect out of the instruction mux, register-file reads and ID/EX\noperand enables.\n\nOn a scratch copy of the current core, this design passed the CoreMark\nCRC and marker checks at 4,190,281 cycles and matched the self-test and\nall three random\n…[5878 of 8950 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f2c43b70a7a95de5]…\ndress to the normal PC\nregister for retry as the recovery pulse expires. This repairs a miss\nwith the existing one-cycle bubble. Preserve the dedicated EX/MEM\neffective-address lane and its forwarding and hold behavior.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype passed the stalled CoreMark markers and CRCs\nin 4,235,641 cycles, 6.45% fewer than the champion. Gowin placement\noptions 0, 1 and 2 measured 64.404, 68.813 and 68.813 MHz. Option 0\nused 3,121 logic LUTs and 846 registers. At the 68.813 MHz median the\nprototype scores 162.46 iterations/s, 7.66% above 150.90. At this cycle\ncount the replacement margin requires a median above 66.856 MHz.\nThe estimate allows for implementation and routing differences.\nThe prototype passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks. Its\nRVFI traces matched the ISS for self-test and all three random\nprograms on ready buses and with both bus stalls enabled. All six\ncocotb suites passed with directed coverage for recovery in both\ndirections, page crossings, traps and stalled recovery fetches.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
