each entry\\nweakly toward its first outcome, then train once per advancing, valid,\\nnontrapping conditional branch. When prediction and training share one\\nentry in a cycle, prediction uses the pre-update state. Predict JAL taken\\nand resolve JALR in EX.\\nUse a shared PC-relative adder for B and JAL immediates and require the\\nspeculative target to be four-byte aligned and below 0x00100000. Select\\nthe predicted successor only when the corresponding\n…[9237 of 12309 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4989b33a37b8e96c]…\nor late-result dependency can stop the predictor from consuming a ready instruction even though the instruction bus is independent. Buffering the predicted path lets fetch use those cycles and gives decode ready instructions to consume during later instruction-bus stalls.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Put a two-entry fall-through queue between the existing page-local predictor and decode. For every accepted imem response, compute the predicted successor at the current fetch PC and advance the fetch cursor. If decode can accept the live response and the queue is empty, bypass it directly to ID/EX. Otherwise retain the instruction, its full PC tag, and the prediction bit that actually steered fetch. Route the oldest entry to decode and shift a valid tail to the head on dequeue. Accept another ready response whenever a slot is free or the head issues that cycle. A full queue with a blocked head holds the fetch cursor and both entries. Use a separate enable for the tail payload so it samples the live packet whenever the ef\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
